db/id 17592186101020 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7f361b6d-88da-56f4-bb4f-1191d0218d94"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101196 :question/problem "A cinema hall can hold $120$ people. The price of each ticket is $$10.$ The cost of maintaining the cinema is made up of a fixed cost of $$400$ plus a cost of $$0.25$ per person. How many tickets should the cinema sell to maximize its profit?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ tickets, the cinema has a revenue (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nR(x)=10\\cdot x=10x\n$$\n\nand a cost (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nC(x)={{400}_{⏟}}_{\\text{fixed cost}}\\,\\,+{{0.25x.}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variable cost}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =10x-(400+0.25x) \\\\  & =9.75x-400.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 120.$ Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=120$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<120.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=9.75.\n$$\n\nWe can see that the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ has no solutions.\n\nOur candidates are $x=0$ and $x=120.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =9.75(0)-400=-400 \\\\ P(120) & =9.75(120)-400=770\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the cinema will have a maximum profit level when it sells $120$ tickets." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186098511 :answer-field/correct 17592186098514 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101018 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-da64bda4-339a-50c3-828b-c79ac0fe60b0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-da64bda4-339a-50c3-828b-c79ac0fe60b0" :question/id #uuid "da64bda4-339a-50c3-828b-c79ac0fe60b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-87576" :question/problem "A daycare center can take care of between $10$ and $20$ children. The fee that parents must pay each month depends on the number of children enrolled in the center. If there are $10$ children enrolled, parents must pay $$40.$ On the other hand, if there are more than $10$ children enrolled, the price drops by $$2$ for each child. All the parents pay the same monthly fee.\n\nHow many children must enroll for the center to maximize its revenue?" :question/worked-solution "First, note that if there are $10+x$ children enrolled, then the monthly fee is $40-2x.$ So, the total revenue is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={{(10+x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{number of children enrolled}}\\times \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{(40-2x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{monthly fee}} \\\\  & =(10+x)(40-2x) \\\\  & =400-20x+40x-2{x}^{2} \\\\  & =400+20x-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the center can only take care between $10$ and $20$ children, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & \\le x+10\\le 20 \\\\ 0 & \\le x\\le 10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ and $x=10$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $0<x<10.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(x)=20-4x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${R}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 20-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x & =20 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=5$ lies in the interval $0<x<10.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=5$ does indeed give a maximum value of $R(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{R}^{″}(x)=-4<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=5$ is indeed a local maximum of $R(x).$ And since $R(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=5$ is a global maximum of $R(x).$ \n\nTherefore, the center must have $10+5=15$ children enrolled to maximize its revenue." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-465ed207-d844-5919-a8df-b87293b0bcf1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-465ed207-d844-5919-a8df-b87293b0bcf1" :answer-field/id #uuid "465ed207-d844-5919-a8df-b87293b0bcf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1d62f0bd-935b-5b00-a2c0-88c9776e3a4f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b9910e6d-7274-5ad8-9582-87f427993b41" "answer-7325bf9e-6b3c-590c-8efe-c6c988414b52" "answer-1d62f0bd-935b-5b00-a2c0-88c9776e3a4f" "answer-bfddd2cc-fb5b-506f-910b-2fc1d8be0dba" "answer-5069e6da-e9ae-553f-8f4a-25dc832f7acc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b9910e6d-7274-5ad8-9582-87f427993b41" :answer/id #uuid "b9910e6d-7274-5ad8-9582-87f427993b41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7325bf9e-6b3c-590c-8efe-c6c988414b52" :answer/id #uuid "7325bf9e-6b3c-590c-8efe-c6c988414b52" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d62f0bd-935b-5b00-a2c0-88c9776e3a4f" :answer/id #uuid "1d62f0bd-935b-5b00-a2c0-88c9776e3a4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bfddd2cc-fb5b-506f-910b-2fc1d8be0dba" :answer/id #uuid "bfddd2cc-fb5b-506f-910b-2fc1d8be0dba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "17" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5069e6da-e9ae-553f-8f4a-25dc832f7acc" :answer/id #uuid "5069e6da-e9ae-553f-8f4a-25dc832f7acc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101019 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4cb999cb-e9bf-5cd0-a21c-a3857e783014" "question-3238715a-ab0d-5fbe-809b-2aa2ec76dbe8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-4cb999cb-e9bf-5cd0-a21c-a3857e783014" :question/id #uuid "4cb999cb-e9bf-5cd0-a21c-a3857e783014" :question/math-academy-id "q-87593" :question/problem "A factory spends $C(x)=100-47x+21{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ thousand dollars per month to manufacture $x$ units of a certain product. The factory sells the product for $$100\\,000$ per unit. Given that the factory can produce between $1$ and $10$ items per month, what is the maximum monthly profit it can make?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units of the product per month, the factory has a monthly revenue (income) of $R(x)=100\\cdot x=100x$ thousand dollars. \n\nTherefore, the profit function (in thousands of dollars) is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =100x-(100-47x+21{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-21{x}^{2}+147x-100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $1\\le x\\le 10.$ Therefore, $x=1$ and $x=10$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $1<x<10.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-42x+147,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-42x+147 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-14x+49 & =0 \\\\ (x-7)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check if $x=7$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=6x-42.\n$$\n\nEvaluating ${P}^{″}(x)$ at the critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{″}(7)=6(7)-42=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich does not confirm or deny that $x=7$ is a maximum of $P(x).$ Therefore, we should consider $x=7$ as one of the candidates.\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,$ $x=7$ and $x=10.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1) & =(1)^{3}-21(1)^{2}+147(1)-100=27 \\\\ P(7) & =(7)^{3}-21(7)^{2}+147(7)-100=243 \\\\ P(10) & =(10)^{3}-21(10)^{2}+147(10)-100=270\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum monthly profit is $270\\cdot $1\\,000=$270\\,000.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cbe295f2-3c78-53d7-a4bc-8cad7afb171c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cbe295f2-3c78-53d7-a4bc-8cad7afb171c" :answer-field/id #uuid "cbe295f2-3c78-53d7-a4bc-8cad7afb171c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6758ee03-03a0-5b60-bde7-55ba18790097" :answer-field/choices ["answer-141d1a25-fba8-5358-bdf7-47d4083d0230" "answer-6758ee03-03a0-5b60-bde7-55ba18790097" "answer-6d3fc12f-fcff-5a8f-956b-4326f0c686ee" "answer-4e64df2e-399d-5b8f-a2f3-3cf2f8c50241" "answer-9619c210-60c8-533d-bf4f-57ac601c6744"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-141d1a25-fba8-5358-bdf7-47d4083d0230" :answer/id #uuid "141d1a25-fba8-5358-bdf7-47d4083d0230" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$243\\,000$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6758ee03-03a0-5b60-bde7-55ba18790097" :answer/id #uuid "6758ee03-03a0-5b60-bde7-55ba18790097" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$270\\,000$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d3fc12f-fcff-5a8f-956b-4326f0c686ee" :answer/id #uuid "6d3fc12f-fcff-5a8f-956b-4326f0c686ee" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$292\\,000$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e64df2e-399d-5b8f-a2f3-3cf2f8c50241" :answer/id #uuid "4e64df2e-399d-5b8f-a2f3-3cf2f8c50241" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$357\\,000$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9619c210-60c8-533d-bf4f-57ac601c6744" :answer/id #uuid "9619c210-60c8-533d-bf4f-57ac601c6744" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$412\\,000$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3238715a-ab0d-5fbe-809b-2aa2ec76dbe8" :question/id #uuid "3238715a-ab0d-5fbe-809b-2aa2ec76dbe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-119056" :question/problem "A company spends $C(x)=0.01{x}^{2}+4x+680$ dollars to produce $x$ units of a certain product. The company sells the product for $$12$ per unit. Given that the company can produce at most $500$ units per month, how many units should the company produce per month to maximize its monthly profit?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units of the product per month, the company has a monthly revenue (income) of $R(x)=12\\cdot x=12x$ dollars. \n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =12x-(0.01{x}^{2}+4x+680) \\\\  & =8x-0.01{x}^{2}-680.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 500.$ \n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<500.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=8-0.02x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 8-0.02x & =0 \\\\ x & =400\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=400$ lies in the interval $0<x<500.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=400$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-0.02<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=400$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=400$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that the company will have a maximum monthly profit when it produces $400$ units per month." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-68dc796d-0c9f-52c2-87ac-381b9f5b4a1e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-68dc796d-0c9f-52c2-87ac-381b9f5b4a1e" :answer-field/id #uuid "68dc796d-0c9f-52c2-87ac-381b9f5b4a1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-989ed443-1a83-563a-80ca-7360914297e1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c7c2e2ef-4e7c-53d2-a226-3d5810d7e74d" "answer-f906ef92-e5c6-577b-a75e-78d163c0e750" "answer-d1f9e17b-f328-54e4-ab9d-74a99d605704" "answer-989ed443-1a83-563a-80ca-7360914297e1" "answer-e9e0e802-95ad-550c-9901-8b89631a6f48"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7c2e2ef-4e7c-53d2-a226-3d5810d7e74d" :answer/id #uuid "c7c2e2ef-4e7c-53d2-a226-3d5810d7e74d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$200$ units per month" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f906ef92-e5c6-577b-a75e-78d163c0e750" :answer/id #uuid "f906ef92-e5c6-577b-a75e-78d163c0e750" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$250$ units per month" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1f9e17b-f328-54e4-ab9d-74a99d605704" :answer/id #uuid "d1f9e17b-f328-54e4-ab9d-74a99d605704" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$40$ units per month" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-989ed443-1a83-563a-80ca-7360914297e1" :answer/id #uuid "989ed443-1a83-563a-80ca-7360914297e1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$400$ units per month" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9e0e802-95ad-550c-9901-8b89631a6f48" :answer/id #uuid "e9e0e802-95ad-550c-9901-8b89631a6f48" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$500$ units per month" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7f361b6d-88da-56f4-bb4f-1191d0218d94" :question/id #uuid "7f361b6d-88da-56f4-bb4f-1191d0218d94" :question/math-academy-id "q-347278" :question/problem "A tutoring center offers private lesson packages. When the price of a package is $$40,$ the center books all $80$ available sessions. For every $$4$ increase in the package price, one fewer session is booked. If the cost of providing each session is $$16,$ what price should the center charge to maximize its profit?\n\nTo maximize profit, the center should charge $$$\n{{field-1}}\nper session." :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nLet $x$ represent the number of unbooked sessions. Then the number of **booked** sessions is $(80-x),$ and the **price per session** is $(40+4x).$\n\nSo, the total revenue is\n\n$$\nR(x)=(80-x)(40+4x).\n$$\n\nExpanding, we get\n\n$$\nR(x)=-4{x}^{2}+280x+3200.\n$$\n\nThe cost of providing each booked session is $$16,$ so the total cost is\n\n$$\nC(x)=16(80-x)=1280-16x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =(-4{x}^{2}+280x+3200)-(1280-16x) \\\\  & =-4{x}^{2}+296x+1920.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given the domain $0\\le x\\le 80.$\n\nTo find the maximum, we take the derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=-8x+296.\n$$\n\nSolving ${P}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get:\n\n$$\nx=37.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{″}(x)=-8<0,$ this point is a maximum. Since $P(x)$ is quadratic, this maximum is global.\n\nNow, if $x=37$ sessions are unbooked, then the price is:\n\n$$\n40+4(37)=188.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to maximize profit, the center should charge $$\\,\\,188$ per session." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bb55bb9f-d933-52e4-9689-fefd9cbfbbcf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bb55bb9f-d933-52e4-9689-fefd9cbfbbcf" :answer-field/id #uuid "bb55bb9f-d933-52e4-9689-fefd9cbfbbcf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-16494579-7656-5c2d-a785-de9c67da7d88" :answer-field/choices ["answer-16494579-7656-5c2d-a785-de9c67da7d88"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16494579-7656-5c2d-a785-de9c67da7d88" :answer/id #uuid "16494579-7656-5c2d-a785-de9c67da7d88" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "188" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-9c2633a4-2f33-5904-a37a-770c0e5abd32" :activity/id #uuid "9c2633a4-2f33-5904-a37a-770c0e5abd32" :activity/title "Optimization Problems in Economics" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048985 :activity/course 17592186046645 :activity/steps ["step-1e9fa964-7041-5ffc-83b1-6b0438a925d8" "step-b36a3f97-5787-5772-8e16-fde45721c2ca" "step-4dd5af7f-a91c-54b8-878b-4a18da96bc9b" "step-c2ce17c2-aa7c-5e08-a132-c77e2f0ad3b4" "step-6df0f32d-2973-5214-b35a-1fd2363c1870"] :activity/first-step "step-1e9fa964-7041-5ffc-83b1-6b0438a925d8" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-1e9fa964-7041-5ffc-83b1-6b0438a925d8" :step/id #uuid "1e9fa964-7041-5ffc-83b1-6b0438a925d8" :step/content 17592186101196 :step/next "step-b36a3f97-5787-5772-8e16-fde45721c2ca" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b36a3f97-5787-5772-8e16-fde45721c2ca" :step/id #uuid "b36a3f97-5787-5772-8e16-fde45721c2ca" :step/content "question-da64bda4-339a-50c3-828b-c79ac0fe60b0" :step/next "step-4dd5af7f-a91c-54b8-878b-4a18da96bc9b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-4dd5af7f-a91c-54b8-878b-4a18da96bc9b" :step/id #uuid "4dd5af7f-a91c-54b8-878b-4a18da96bc9b" :step/content "question-4cb999cb-e9bf-5cd0-a21c-a3857e783014" :step/next "step-c2ce17c2-aa7c-5e08-a132-c77e2f0ad3b4" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c2ce17c2-aa7c-5e08-a132-c77e2f0ad3b4" :step/id #uuid "c2ce17c2-aa7c-5e08-a132-c77e2f0ad3b4" :step/content "question-3238715a-ab0d-5fbe-809b-2aa2ec76dbe8" :step/next "step-6df0f32d-2973-5214-b35a-1fd2363c1870" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6df0f32d-2973-5214-b35a-1fd2363c1870" :step/id #uuid "6df0f32d-2973-5214-b35a-1fd2363c1870" :step/content "question-7f361b6d-88da-56f4-bb4f-1191d0218d94" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059686 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-44b12f59-5bfc-5de8-98fe-816ee27220b4" "question-6d153978-db48-529b-877e-2cfabb3e0039"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059514 :question/problem "The angle $\\theta =  \\dfrac {8\\pi}3$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\dfrac {8\\pi}3$ by *subtracting* integer multiples of $2\\pi$ until we get a coterminal angle that lies in the range $[0, 2\\pi).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8\\pi }{3}-2\\pi =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the angle $\\dfrac {2\\pi}3$ is coterminal with $\\dfrac {8\\pi}3.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/23/2360ed202deeca403c4631c319b69df30a9f0268b7ef270a8e436103c0ac3edb.png)\n\nWe see that $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ lies in quadrant II. Therefore, $\\dfrac{8\\pi}{3}$ also lies in this quadrant." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059685 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1327ffff-07aa-5bea-93ff-453e6c9d98a5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059513 :question/problem "The angle $\\theta =  -435^\\circ$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-435^\\circ$ by *adding* integer multiples of $360^\\circ$ until we get a coterminal angle that lies in the range $[0^\\circ, 360^\\circ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{435}^{∘}+{360}^{∘} & =-{75}^{∘} \\\\ -{75}^{∘}+{360}^{∘} & ={285}^{∘}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the angle $285^\\circ$ is coterminal with $-435^\\circ.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/4f/4f3fc79c2fc99478c407a63352d9113ef9cbef6a93a6feb62bd284d5f443bed5.png)\n\nWe see that $285^\\circ$ lies in quadrant IV. Therefore, $-435^\\circ$ also lies in this quadrant." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059683 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a22714ba-933f-5202-894c-587dd0012788" "question-a369ef6f-b58e-5e3b-af34-1de431b7ed56"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059511 :question/problem "Which angles are coterminal with $240^\\circ?$\n\n$$\n600^\\circ, \\quad 300^\\circ, \\quad -480^\\circ.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta,$ we add and subtract integer multiples of $360^\\circ\\mathbin{.}$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $360^\\circ$ to $240^\\circ\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{240}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={600}^{∘}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no answer choices larger than $600^\\circ,$ so we can stop here.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $360^\\circ$ from $240^\\circ\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{240}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} & =-{120}^{∘} \\\\ {240}^{∘}-2\\cdot ({360}^{∘}) & =-{480}^{∘}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no answer choices smaller than $-480^\\circ,$ so we can stop here.\n\nSo, of the given angles, the coterminal angles are $600^\\circ$ and $-480^\\circ.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059684 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a6cfd9d2-1fde-532f-8824-00cee2a76d89" "question-af0faf42-b43a-5224-af78-09641aa5c63d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059512 :question/problem "Which angle is coterminal with $\\dfrac {3 \\pi} {5}?$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4\\pi}{5}, \\quad -\\dfrac{7\\pi}{5}, \\quad -\\dfrac{11\\pi}{5}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta$ (in radians), we add and subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi.$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $2\\pi$ to $\\dfrac {3 \\pi} {5}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\pi }{5}+2\\pi =\\frac{13\\pi }{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no answer choices larger than $\\dfrac {13 \\pi} {5},$ so we can stop here.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $2\\pi$ from $\\dfrac {3 \\pi} {5}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\pi }{5}-2\\pi  & =-\\frac{7\\pi }{5}\\,✓ \\\\ \\frac{3\\pi }{5}-2\\cdot (2\\pi ) & =-\\frac{17\\pi }{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no answer choices smaller than $-\\dfrac {17 \\pi} {5},$ so we can stop here.\n\nSo, of the given angles, the only coterminal angle is $-\\dfrac{7\\pi}{5}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-44b12f59-5bfc-5de8-98fe-816ee27220b4" :question/id #uuid "44b12f59-5bfc-5de8-98fe-816ee27220b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-64674" :question/problem "The angle $\\theta ={1032}^{∘}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with ${1032}^{∘}$ by *subtracting* integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}).$\n\n- ${1032}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}={672}^{∘}$\n\n- ${1032}^{∘}-2\\cdot {360}^{∘}={312}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle ${312}^{∘}$ is coterminal with ${1032}^{∘}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/6d/6db0d45744872518e73e249bc793e48792202c100bf42739fc889febb0fd6c32.png)\n\nWe see that ${312}^{∘}$ lies in quadrant IV. Therefore, ${1032}^{∘}$ also lies in this quadrant." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9a1e73fc-4b88-5be7-88c3-8ba6bc02b059"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9a1e73fc-4b88-5be7-88c3-8ba6bc02b059" :answer-field/id #uuid "9a1e73fc-4b88-5be7-88c3-8ba6bc02b059" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b73363ac-c3cc-52cf-ac01-aa97731a63c3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b6751d47-e997-5e08-845d-d41e40b5f80d" "answer-9f1199c3-536c-5b9f-afd9-c28bb4898145" "answer-c49d0bec-f41b-5b98-8625-bb6185fbaf75" "answer-4b800297-4e6e-5886-b40a-55cf266d1c00" "answer-b73363ac-c3cc-52cf-ac01-aa97731a63c3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6751d47-e997-5e08-845d-d41e40b5f80d" :answer/id #uuid "b6751d47-e997-5e08-845d-d41e40b5f80d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Between quadrants II and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f1199c3-536c-5b9f-afd9-c28bb4898145" :answer/id #uuid "9f1199c3-536c-5b9f-afd9-c28bb4898145" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c49d0bec-f41b-5b98-8625-bb6185fbaf75" :answer/id #uuid "c49d0bec-f41b-5b98-8625-bb6185fbaf75" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b800297-4e6e-5886-b40a-55cf266d1c00" :answer/id #uuid "4b800297-4e6e-5886-b40a-55cf266d1c00" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b73363ac-c3cc-52cf-ac01-aa97731a63c3" :answer/id #uuid "b73363ac-c3cc-52cf-ac01-aa97731a63c3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "IV" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6d153978-db48-529b-877e-2cfabb3e0039" :question/id #uuid "6d153978-db48-529b-877e-2cfabb3e0039" :question/math-academy-id "q-64828" :question/problem "The angle $\\theta =\\frac{19\\pi }{4}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{19\\pi }{4}$ by *subtracting* integer multiples of $2\\pi $ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n- $\\frac{19\\pi }{4}-2\\pi =\\frac{11\\pi }{4}$\n\n- $\\frac{19\\pi }{4}-2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle $\\frac{19\\pi }{4}$ is coterminal with $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/d0/d00b1363ef23bfd1e84ae968e1dc6ec4e36aa7f45d469c653b9f930cb0705cdf.png)\n\nWe see that $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ lies in quadrant II. Therefore, $\\frac{19\\pi }{4}$ also lies in this quadrant." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c14d592a-a58d-5792-a27c-756cd1ffc7cd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c14d592a-a58d-5792-a27c-756cd1ffc7cd" :answer-field/id #uuid "c14d592a-a58d-5792-a27c-756cd1ffc7cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6aef0caa-43e1-5fb3-bc94-36cf76ee550b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ea12ce20-c200-5e65-8109-bda4f6295bf0" "answer-6ec509d9-b4bb-5e15-b06f-e0dc0490b1da" "answer-6aef0caa-43e1-5fb3-bc94-36cf76ee550b" "answer-3e5318bb-61f3-5058-b0bd-5122c25f1bbd" "answer-3b4e78cd-5ed8-570d-bf24-7a6c0b064d5d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea12ce20-c200-5e65-8109-bda4f6295bf0" :answer/id #uuid "ea12ce20-c200-5e65-8109-bda4f6295bf0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Between quadrants III and IV" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ec509d9-b4bb-5e15-b06f-e0dc0490b1da" :answer/id #uuid "6ec509d9-b4bb-5e15-b06f-e0dc0490b1da" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6aef0caa-43e1-5fb3-bc94-36cf76ee550b" :answer/id #uuid "6aef0caa-43e1-5fb3-bc94-36cf76ee550b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e5318bb-61f3-5058-b0bd-5122c25f1bbd" :answer/id #uuid "3e5318bb-61f3-5058-b0bd-5122c25f1bbd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b4e78cd-5ed8-570d-bf24-7a6c0b064d5d" :answer/id #uuid "3b4e78cd-5ed8-570d-bf24-7a6c0b064d5d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "IV" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1327ffff-07aa-5bea-93ff-453e6c9d98a5" :question/id #uuid "1327ffff-07aa-5bea-93ff-453e6c9d98a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-124720" :question/problem "The angle $\\theta =-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}$ by *adding* integer multiples of $2\\pi $ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n- $-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}+2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{5}$\n\n- $-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}+2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{9\\pi }{5}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle $-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}$ is coterminal with $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/4a/4a6bb0dd4991fe1a00bfedc7814f27c4a36b4114fa027673ed9cd03856fcc0d5.png)\n\nWe see that $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$ lies in quadrant IV. Therefore, $-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}$ also lies in this quadrant." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8ef2b863-b1a2-5dec-97a2-44f43a865e09"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8ef2b863-b1a2-5dec-97a2-44f43a865e09" :answer-field/id #uuid "8ef2b863-b1a2-5dec-97a2-44f43a865e09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8ac9ccee-5559-5bd5-9840-ea449a11fab2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c15bb5ff-a135-58be-85f2-e48382a26409" "answer-b1377c36-6065-59dc-9ecf-a0684bb8d804" "answer-c8dab11c-30b4-5a72-b17b-700c26dba4bc" "answer-14499200-08a0-52d8-a84d-ee93f9bba9b0" "answer-8ac9ccee-5559-5bd5-9840-ea449a11fab2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c15bb5ff-a135-58be-85f2-e48382a26409" :answer/id #uuid "c15bb5ff-a135-58be-85f2-e48382a26409" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Between quadrants II and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1377c36-6065-59dc-9ecf-a0684bb8d804" :answer/id #uuid "b1377c36-6065-59dc-9ecf-a0684bb8d804" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c8dab11c-30b4-5a72-b17b-700c26dba4bc" :answer/id #uuid "c8dab11c-30b4-5a72-b17b-700c26dba4bc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14499200-08a0-52d8-a84d-ee93f9bba9b0" :answer/id #uuid "14499200-08a0-52d8-a84d-ee93f9bba9b0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ac9ccee-5559-5bd5-9840-ea449a11fab2" :answer/id #uuid "8ac9ccee-5559-5bd5-9840-ea449a11fab2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "IV" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a22714ba-933f-5202-894c-587dd0012788" :question/id #uuid "a22714ba-933f-5202-894c-587dd0012788" :question/math-academy-id "q-124736" :question/problem "Which angle is coterminal with $-{202}^{∘}?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $ (in degrees), we add and subtract integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ to $-{202}^{∘}$:\n\n- $-{202}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={158}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\n- $-{202}^{∘}+2\\cdot {360}^{∘}={518}^{∘}$\n\nThere are no answer choices larger than ${518}^{∘},$ so we can stop here.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ from $-{202}^{∘}$:\n\n- $-{202}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{562}^{∘}$\n\n- $-{202}^{∘}-2\\cdot {360}^{∘}=-{922}^{∘}$\n\nThere are no answer choices smaller than $-{922}^{∘},$ so we can stop here.\n\nSo the correct answer is ${158}^{∘}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fbcaf346-14fa-5491-a96b-990906294b58"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fbcaf346-14fa-5491-a96b-990906294b58" :answer-field/id #uuid "fbcaf346-14fa-5491-a96b-990906294b58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8f4a55b6-48c9-52ea-a3cd-36bfb3dff46e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-76e052bd-8901-553b-935a-bfdce70be9ea" "answer-500db5a2-2c49-50cf-aa2a-79d3f0ba6bb4" "answer-8f4a55b6-48c9-52ea-a3cd-36bfb3dff46e" "answer-0ef067df-a2f2-5923-854f-168b758fa2b7" "answer-421f2dac-dcd8-5232-a679-824545657b9c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76e052bd-8901-553b-935a-bfdce70be9ea" :answer/id #uuid "76e052bd-8901-553b-935a-bfdce70be9ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{538}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-500db5a2-2c49-50cf-aa2a-79d3f0ba6bb4" :answer/id #uuid "500db5a2-2c49-50cf-aa2a-79d3f0ba6bb4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{912}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f4a55b6-48c9-52ea-a3cd-36bfb3dff46e" :answer/id #uuid "8f4a55b6-48c9-52ea-a3cd-36bfb3dff46e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{158}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ef067df-a2f2-5923-854f-168b758fa2b7" :answer/id #uuid "0ef067df-a2f2-5923-854f-168b758fa2b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{212}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-421f2dac-dcd8-5232-a679-824545657b9c" :answer/id #uuid "421f2dac-dcd8-5232-a679-824545657b9c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{502}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a369ef6f-b58e-5e3b-af34-1de431b7ed56" :question/id #uuid "a369ef6f-b58e-5e3b-af34-1de431b7ed56" :question/math-academy-id "q-1679" :question/problem "Which of the following angles are coterminal with $\\theta =-{390}^{∘}?$\n\nI. $-{30}^{∘}$\n\nII. ${305}^{∘}$\n\nIII. $-{750}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $, we add and subtract integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}:$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ to $-{390}^{∘}$:\n\n- $-{390}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}=-{30}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\n- $-{390}^{∘}+2\\cdot {360}^{∘}={330}^{∘}$\n\nOf the given values, there is no angle greater than ${330}^{∘}.$ So we can stop there.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ from $-{390}^{∘}$\n\n- $-{390}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{750}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nOf the given angles, only $-{30}^{∘}$ and $-{750}^{∘}$ are coterminal with $-{390}^{∘}.$\n\nSo the correct answer is I and III only." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-75cb9fcb-fa4a-5548-b35c-bb9a570cfcd2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-75cb9fcb-fa4a-5548-b35c-bb9a570cfcd2" :answer-field/id #uuid "75cb9fcb-fa4a-5548-b35c-bb9a570cfcd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-299915e8-7595-5560-8759-70bd258f92d8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-299915e8-7595-5560-8759-70bd258f92d8" "answer-03bbb6f4-95c4-59d2-90ba-0d83c32db390" "answer-d155f444-c7be-5482-8698-ef7013570444" "answer-53f5d72f-c59f-552e-87f2-dd36410b9459" "answer-d5847306-0c13-5de4-803c-bbb31889a2f8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-299915e8-7595-5560-8759-70bd258f92d8" :answer/id #uuid "299915e8-7595-5560-8759-70bd258f92d8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03bbb6f4-95c4-59d2-90ba-0d83c32db390" :answer/id #uuid "03bbb6f4-95c4-59d2-90ba-0d83c32db390" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d155f444-c7be-5482-8698-ef7013570444" :answer/id #uuid "d155f444-c7be-5482-8698-ef7013570444" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-53f5d72f-c59f-552e-87f2-dd36410b9459" :answer/id #uuid "53f5d72f-c59f-552e-87f2-dd36410b9459" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d5847306-0c13-5de4-803c-bbb31889a2f8" :answer/id #uuid "d5847306-0c13-5de4-803c-bbb31889a2f8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a6cfd9d2-1fde-532f-8824-00cee2a76d89" :question/id #uuid "a6cfd9d2-1fde-532f-8824-00cee2a76d89" :question/math-academy-id "q-22983" :question/problem "Which of the following angles are coterminal with $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}?$\n\nI. $\\frac{8\\pi }{3}$\n\nII. $\\frac{14\\pi }{3}$\n\nIII. $-\\frac{10\\pi }{3}$" :question/worked-solution "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $, we add and subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi :$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $2\\pi $ to $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$:\n\n- $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\pi =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}\\,✓$\n\n- $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{14\\pi }{3}\\,✓$\n\nOf the given values, there is no angle greater than $\\frac{14\\pi }{3}.$ So, we can stop there.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $2\\pi $ from $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$:\n\n- $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-2\\pi =-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$\n\n- $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-2\\cdot (2\\pi )=-\\frac{10\\pi }{3}\\,✓$\n\nOf the given angles, all three are coterminal with $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nSo the correct answer is I, II, and III." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d4bb506e-820b-5bd7-9c88-97a9f7dff0f6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d4bb506e-820b-5bd7-9c88-97a9f7dff0f6" :answer-field/id #uuid "d4bb506e-820b-5bd7-9c88-97a9f7dff0f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-724de728-6c17-5a4b-a32b-fb8debf6f9fc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-36162952-7ce9-532c-b904-12a3ca70ccd1" "answer-a8891c14-44aa-5ce5-b3eb-d54ddda7534a" "answer-ad7c0136-e41d-5ea0-9952-8959d50e85b8" "answer-724de728-6c17-5a4b-a32b-fb8debf6f9fc" "answer-6929c1e6-6549-5888-b7cb-ab779bbc9769"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36162952-7ce9-532c-b904-12a3ca70ccd1" :answer/id #uuid "36162952-7ce9-532c-b904-12a3ca70ccd1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a8891c14-44aa-5ce5-b3eb-d54ddda7534a" :answer/id #uuid "a8891c14-44aa-5ce5-b3eb-d54ddda7534a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad7c0136-e41d-5ea0-9952-8959d50e85b8" :answer/id #uuid "ad7c0136-e41d-5ea0-9952-8959d50e85b8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-724de728-6c17-5a4b-a32b-fb8debf6f9fc" :answer/id #uuid "724de728-6c17-5a4b-a32b-fb8debf6f9fc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6929c1e6-6549-5888-b7cb-ab779bbc9769" :answer/id #uuid "6929c1e6-6549-5888-b7cb-ab779bbc9769" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-af0faf42-b43a-5224-af78-09641aa5c63d" :question/id #uuid "af0faf42-b43a-5224-af78-09641aa5c63d" :question/math-academy-id "q-124757" :question/problem "Which angle is coterminal with $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $ (in radians), we add and subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi :$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $2\\pi $ to $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$:\n\n- $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}+2\\pi =\\frac{19\\pi }{5}$\n\n- $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}+2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{29\\pi }{5}\\,✓$\n\nThere are no answer choices larger than $\\frac{29\\pi }{5},$ so we can stop here.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $2\\pi $ from $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$:\n\n- $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{5}$\n\n- $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}-2\\cdot (2\\pi )=-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}$\n\n- $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}-3\\cdot (2\\pi )=-\\frac{21\\pi }{5}$\n\nThere are no answer choices smaller than $-\\frac{21\\pi }{5},$ so we can stop here.\n\nSo the correct answer is $\\frac{29\\pi }{5}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-657c9380-e503-5306-a566-c3d47bda5bce"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-657c9380-e503-5306-a566-c3d47bda5bce" :answer-field/id #uuid "657c9380-e503-5306-a566-c3d47bda5bce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1c76cf94-8053-5e64-aa82-a82122cb96a4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c9c09866-fa8a-5a31-b2d1-c484025a37d9" "answer-d6370293-ebf8-5491-947c-6d38067aa92e" "answer-e422c3e3-c1ab-5384-90f4-4b7d20e5cd4a" "answer-36f02b83-07f0-58d0-bfbb-80ef980ceea1" "answer-1c76cf94-8053-5e64-aa82-a82122cb96a4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c9c09866-fa8a-5a31-b2d1-c484025a37d9" :answer/id #uuid "c9c09866-fa8a-5a31-b2d1-c484025a37d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{17\\pi }{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6370293-ebf8-5491-947c-6d38067aa92e" :answer/id #uuid "d6370293-ebf8-5491-947c-6d38067aa92e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e422c3e3-c1ab-5384-90f4-4b7d20e5cd4a" :answer/id #uuid "e422c3e3-c1ab-5384-90f4-4b7d20e5cd4a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{12\\pi }{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36f02b83-07f0-58d0-bfbb-80ef980ceea1" :answer/id #uuid "36f02b83-07f0-58d0-bfbb-80ef980ceea1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{23\\pi }{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c76cf94-8053-5e64-aa82-a82122cb96a4" :answer/id #uuid "1c76cf94-8053-5e64-aa82-a82122cb96a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{29\\pi }{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186060901 :tutorial/content "All the angles we have constructed so far have been inside the range ![](images/fa/fa8919b9a8968214967b7c4b868b1eb64a95c1d0d2068bd312a4a4bb11ad4e6f.svg) However, we can also construct angles outside this range.\n\nThe way we do this involves **coterminal angles**. Coterminal angles are different angles that appear as the same angle in the coordinate plane. For example, the angles ![](images/d2/d22511f2212b05e9408f52f8da19a125897737f033bb64f480780f2f024b2091.svg) and ![](images/6f/6f3e3ccd469bd4b7cf2baf7a17cad64bb11f3f6f2d202e79362b8c44df1fcdd2.svg) are coterminal because a rotation of ![](images/6f/6f3e3ccd469bd4b7cf2baf7a17cad64bb11f3f6f2d202e79362b8c44df1fcdd2.svg) is the same as the rotation of ![](images/d5/d5c42dddda6dd2e3707f543621c5ab3ef3557e26aa8e938ffddc686917238fdd.svg) In general, whenever we add or subtract a multiple of ![](images/43/431e60581c842f0bf0e2554ccfbe65001d79fd5493de301c6889f8237304b725.svg) we get a coterminal angle.\n\nThe standard way in which we draw a **positive** angle ![](images/06/06e47e2878930095abe73547809f3c9f87be3cd9b3b9069ca582970b60598840.svg) in the coordinate plane is as follows:\n\n- Subtract integer multiples of ![](images/6f/6f3e3ccd469bd4b7cf2baf7a17cad64bb11f3f6f2d202e79362b8c44df1fcdd2.svg) until we get a coterminal angle that lies in the range ![](images/29/29577be589a8a6614c131d995797fa1224c1df2f6b538a669e5fba718edb926b.svg)\n\n- Draw the resulting angle in the coordinate plane.\n\nFor example, to represent the angle ![](images/fa/fae5f1a011f55464bbc26a61e49aa6c4b2e2b9fb2076370a05f35c00de3a7a29.svg) in the coordinate plane, we first subtract ![](images/29/2903c04e0275e76d718eff7ae8277ecb8b5404777225212dca2507b93907ab41.svg)\n\n![](images/3b/3b3bf36f7e1ccf241036659c484ffa0dd17884f8044f6bd6faf6240764552c41.svg)\n\nThe result, ![](images/73/73c6bd81124f4f9c92e0d8ed37449d8dd5be227f9e4b05b2f7953d28ddff7d6c.svg) is a coterminal angle that lies in the range ![](images/29/29577be589a8a6614c131d995797fa1224c1df2f6b538a669e5fba718edb926b.svg) So, to draw the angle ![](images/63/637324a8ad2f6d52227a2fe46d938a816aab3de3d50e0f9ee035f602537e2091.svg) we draw the coterminal angle ![](images/33/33cc1d85e29c1ae4ce3b019b0e49a5dba77efefa6f405590a91fce42f504baf3.svg) in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/bb/bbeb3dfdcb56e4f7f1a39010dc798af004f3ab5ee843f653e86f80fe9974dd30.png)\n\n**Note:** For every multiple of ![](images/4b/4b6a99f552f4ddb574063b55a576d5ba9d4a8fbc2be8b87c8eb11dfee8331c34.svg) we subtract, we make one complete rotation counter-clockwise around the origin. A rotation of ![](images/4b/4b6a99f552f4ddb574063b55a576d5ba9d4a8fbc2be8b87c8eb11dfee8331c34.svg) is the same as the rotation of ![](images/57/5792ebfa34e0824d805857dcb2467c1c23d3786ae5a5bad16b0c1f6616b6b0bf.svg) so it doesn't contribute to our final angle.\n\nHere, we subtracted ![](images/4b/4b6a99f552f4ddb574063b55a576d5ba9d4a8fbc2be8b87c8eb11dfee8331c34.svg) a single time, so we make a single rotation.\n\n![](images/ac/ac16cf651b6f5e16d3f31418646388dac16384c4824e0095bf9a925c13b1ea77.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186060903 :tutorial/content "The method for constructing a *negative* angle ![](images/06/06e47e2878930095abe73547809f3c9f87be3cd9b3b9069ca582970b60598840.svg) outside the range ![](images/3b/3bbae843070f7afe0fc1c253cc8dd663ed4571cc5d250bebae728dd3298cee8a.svg) is similar to the method for positive angles, but with one main difference: we *add* multiples of ![](images/43/431e60581c842f0bf0e2554ccfbe65001d79fd5493de301c6889f8237304b725.svg) instead of subtracting. So the steps are:\n\n- Add integer multiples of ![](images/6f/6f3e3ccd469bd4b7cf2baf7a17cad64bb11f3f6f2d202e79362b8c44df1fcdd2.svg) until we get a coterminal angle that lies in the range ![](images/29/29577be589a8a6614c131d995797fa1224c1df2f6b538a669e5fba718edb926b.svg)\n\n- Draw the resulting angle in the coordinate plane.\n\nFor example, we can represent the angle ![](images/78/78826f360e99c31e2e800e0691e6770af9a57442c1b09212bb94f3df8a7a7e6c.svg) as follows:\n\n![](images/bc/bcc5c8dedaeca80ac8c48f4d5d18aa3666e87b5be942f1a49d187f99ade28435.svg)\n\nNow, we draw the angle ![](images/20/20615d4147fe63007acc775caaa7d175edca7e0717a9e1224b4a66d490df2316.svg) in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/78/785973780f0ed538c0a0f430a402c25fb562a7308911f1d5cecc958da02b99f2.png)\n\nAgain, for every multiple of ![](images/4b/4b6a99f552f4ddb574063b55a576d5ba9d4a8fbc2be8b87c8eb11dfee8331c34.svg) that we add, we make one complete rotation around the origin clockwise. A rotation of ![](images/4b/4b6a99f552f4ddb574063b55a576d5ba9d4a8fbc2be8b87c8eb11dfee8331c34.svg) is the same as the rotation of ![](images/57/5792ebfa34e0824d805857dcb2467c1c23d3786ae5a5bad16b0c1f6616b6b0bf.svg) so it doesn't contribute to our final angle." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186060902 :tutorial/content "To find a coterminal angle of a given angle ![](images/1f/1f216b45120e801bbdcf2c3f165ac341a7dd0bd305f87381647b205831b06725.svg) we rotate ![](images/6f/6f3e3ccd469bd4b7cf2baf7a17cad64bb11f3f6f2d202e79362b8c44df1fcdd2.svg) clockwise or counter-clockwise from ![](images/b9/b98533dee87a7bd8ed4d593a2aabd517621d6ab96e40a5b2ab4a8ac1923ddac5.svg) We could do this again and again, each time looping by ![](images/6f/6f3e3ccd469bd4b7cf2baf7a17cad64bb11f3f6f2d202e79362b8c44df1fcdd2.svg) to find an infinite number of coterminal angles:\n![](images/bc/bcd7ec5620afdf6f142437b0a0aedcddcee4678309d9d2328da3892eb0034730.svg)\n\nFor instance, the angles ![](images/ed/ed05bb3b845471f7526114d38f3c1a525aeb653d50a50b3ebe0457266e275d5f.svg) ![](images/94/9476642d5d3f3e418c71f7b062f33f894fa648e3c0cee6a15fc359b43889c19a.svg) ![](images/94/94f4422d3745b4401e7c9517c9e08382bb4d4af59a4f9ef164500530b530a525.svg) and ![](images/98/98e07f13eb10e759924519f7b068aa9f9768dcd7c362088b89f1831c886a3a2d.svg) are all coterminal.\n\n  \n\n![](images/c2/c2949532fb08722c3dbaaad3cd971ae2c3d3e7d7606f051da6dccd2bc3cfecc6.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059936 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198390 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-50078542-09f5-53cd-a63a-27e043674d5d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-50078542-09f5-53cd-a63a-27e043674d5d" :question/id #uuid "50078542-09f5-53cd-a63a-27e043674d5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162562" :question/problem "![](images/cf/cf90197ac99a066db75154200de4cbee2494fef15deed6be7b7c4cd501ae8f26.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨4x{y}^{2}{z}^{3},\\,0,\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $x$-component of $F=⟨4x{y}^{2}{z}^{3},\\,0,\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $x$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the front and back sides. \n\n![](images/dc/dc37a067eb8070ecbdfea53c1ede3e221e1873a192b50fcbf07234f33c183787.png)\n\n  \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the front surface. On this surface, we have $x=3$ for $0\\le y\\le 2$ and $0\\le z\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x=3$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨4x{y}^{2}{z}^{3},\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12{y}^{2}{z}^{3},\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =12{y}^{2}{z}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}12{y}^{2}{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}12{y}^{2}{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}12{y}^{2}{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[4{y}^{3}{z}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}32{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[8{z}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the back surface. On this surface, we have $x=0$ for $0\\le y\\le 2$ and $0\\le z\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨4x{y}^{2}{z}^{3},\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =8+0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8118e8d0-ad36-5eed-a94c-c49d91624022"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8118e8d0-ad36-5eed-a94c-c49d91624022" :answer-field/id #uuid "8118e8d0-ad36-5eed-a94c-c49d91624022" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a6c9610c-4027-5e7b-a8f2-534eb909848c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-38c6cf47-a825-5070-89ae-ba194b490421" "answer-1242a293-4ae5-5fa1-bfa5-f75baf035973" "answer-753675fc-41c9-57ed-b147-8dbaf347a205" "answer-570a9294-3afd-5d27-ace4-dabede751046" "answer-a6c9610c-4027-5e7b-a8f2-534eb909848c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38c6cf47-a825-5070-89ae-ba194b490421" :answer/id #uuid "38c6cf47-a825-5070-89ae-ba194b490421" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1242a293-4ae5-5fa1-bfa5-f75baf035973" :answer/id #uuid "1242a293-4ae5-5fa1-bfa5-f75baf035973" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-753675fc-41c9-57ed-b147-8dbaf347a205" :answer/id #uuid "753675fc-41c9-57ed-b147-8dbaf347a205" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-570a9294-3afd-5d27-ace4-dabede751046" :answer/id #uuid "570a9294-3afd-5d27-ace4-dabede751046" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6c9610c-4027-5e7b-a8f2-534eb909848c" :answer/id #uuid "a6c9610c-4027-5e7b-a8f2-534eb909848c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-d03962de-53d8-58b6-98cc-85b49115e6d9" :activity/id #uuid "d03962de-53d8-58b6-98cc-85b49115e6d9" :activity/title "Flux Through Closed Surfaces" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049315 :activity/course 17592186046645 :activity/steps ["step-52481592-db04-5beb-9a44-7dfde1d1ecfd" "step-abb83d5b-b9f4-5ee1-8479-b852400c679a" "step-39301e03-b48e-51b6-bb45-a99701503350" "step-87f36f5a-67a0-55ce-9ac0-719dbb07135b" "step-f03fe234-508d-516c-bf47-5e1dd4eafb5f"] :activity/first-step "step-52481592-db04-5beb-9a44-7dfde1d1ecfd" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-52481592-db04-5beb-9a44-7dfde1d1ecfd" :step/id #uuid "52481592-db04-5beb-9a44-7dfde1d1ecfd" :step/content 17592186278422 :step/next "step-abb83d5b-b9f4-5ee1-8479-b852400c679a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-abb83d5b-b9f4-5ee1-8479-b852400c679a" :step/id #uuid "abb83d5b-b9f4-5ee1-8479-b852400c679a" :step/content 17592186278430 :step/next "step-39301e03-b48e-51b6-bb45-a99701503350" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-39301e03-b48e-51b6-bb45-a99701503350" :step/id #uuid "39301e03-b48e-51b6-bb45-a99701503350" :step/content 17592186278436 :step/next "step-87f36f5a-67a0-55ce-9ac0-719dbb07135b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-87f36f5a-67a0-55ce-9ac0-719dbb07135b" :step/id #uuid "87f36f5a-67a0-55ce-9ac0-719dbb07135b" :step/content 17592186278441 :step/next "step-f03fe234-508d-516c-bf47-5e1dd4eafb5f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f03fe234-508d-516c-bf47-5e1dd4eafb5f" :step/id #uuid "f03fe234-508d-516c-bf47-5e1dd4eafb5f" :step/content "question-50078542-09f5-53cd-a63a-27e043674d5d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087880 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ab9ad28a-3195-5102-ad22-90c2dc511297" "question-0d6da41c-2674-5be8-ab4e-fb7795b804a5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087431 :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = x^2,$ what is $f'(x)$ according to the definition of the derivative?" :question/worked-solution "The derivative $f '(x)$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to 0} \\dfrac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} .\n$$\n\nSince $f(x) = x^2$,  we have that $f(x+h) = (x+h)^2.$ Hence,  we get\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f'(x)=\\lim_{h\\to 0} \\dfrac{(x+h)^2-x^2}{h} .\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087881 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-779ee099-0fc5-5dca-bd3b-ce3793ba0e8c" "question-a35127e9-e5f3-508e-9d5a-b3279aa78b1a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087432 :question/problem "Given $y =x^2,$ calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ using the limit of a difference quotient." :question/worked-solution "We let $y=f(x)$ and apply the formula for the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(x+h)^{2}-{x}^{2}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xh+{h}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2xh+{h}^{2}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{2xh}{h}+\\frac{{h}^{2}}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(2x+h) \\\\  & =2x+0 \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-ab9ad28a-3195-5102-ad22-90c2dc511297" :question/id #uuid "ab9ad28a-3195-5102-ad22-90c2dc511297" :question/math-academy-id "q-101957" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=2x+1$, what is ${f}^{′}(x)$ according to the definition of the derivative?" :question/worked-solution "The derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)=2x+1$,  we have that $f(x+h)=2(x+h)+1.$ Hence,  we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{2(x+h)+1-(2x+1)}{h}).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f4d0ff0d-7860-5a8a-a6e6-4fb8f62ae866"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f4d0ff0d-7860-5a8a-a6e6-4fb8f62ae866" :answer-field/id #uuid "f4d0ff0d-7860-5a8a-a6e6-4fb8f62ae866" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7ab95eba-e6f1-50b3-93cf-0ac8c24f9109" :answer-field/choices ["answer-957167b9-3ca4-5394-9fd5-5962be3124cc" "answer-31269d34-bf9b-5beb-8795-1803e3e8f873" "answer-7ab95eba-e6f1-50b3-93cf-0ac8c24f9109" "answer-daeffdac-cd8f-5ed5-9255-0868e62e563e" "answer-b02f15e2-ec30-5b7e-8dd6-d998fa88fd43"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-957167b9-3ca4-5394-9fd5-5962be3124cc" :answer/id #uuid "957167b9-3ca4-5394-9fd5-5962be3124cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{(x+h)+x}{h})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31269d34-bf9b-5beb-8795-1803e3e8f873" :answer/id #uuid "31269d34-bf9b-5beb-8795-1803e3e8f873" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{(x+h)-(2x+1)}{h})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ab95eba-e6f1-50b3-93cf-0ac8c24f9109" :answer/id #uuid "7ab95eba-e6f1-50b3-93cf-0ac8c24f9109" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{2(x+h)+1-(2x+1)}{h})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-daeffdac-cd8f-5ed5-9255-0868e62e563e" :answer/id #uuid "daeffdac-cd8f-5ed5-9255-0868e62e563e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{2x+1-(x+h)}{h})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b02f15e2-ec30-5b7e-8dd6-d998fa88fd43" :answer/id #uuid "b02f15e2-ec30-5b7e-8dd6-d998fa88fd43" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{2x+1-(x+h+1)}{h})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0d6da41c-2674-5be8-ab4e-fb7795b804a5" :question/id #uuid "0d6da41c-2674-5be8-ab4e-fb7795b804a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-101958" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=-7x$, what is ${f}^{′}(x)$ according to the definition of the derivative?" :question/worked-solution "The derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)=-7x$,  we have that $f(x+h)=-7(x+h).$ Hence,  we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{-7(x+h)-(-7x)}{h}).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a05863a1-93f7-5544-886b-fd004cc1deef"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a05863a1-93f7-5544-886b-fd004cc1deef" :answer-field/id #uuid "a05863a1-93f7-5544-886b-fd004cc1deef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c3af6ada-214b-5c5d-8e42-88d836757eb4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-39ae355b-9bd5-55ce-b14f-de747087c884" "answer-aaa427b3-244e-5498-9b61-671f781cd044" "answer-c3af6ada-214b-5c5d-8e42-88d836757eb4" "answer-fee23737-7fe3-58db-a3d3-3228bf0805d8" "answer-33e3ce1d-b9dc-5b4f-9df8-3667487f8eec"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-39ae355b-9bd5-55ce-b14f-de747087c884" :answer/id #uuid "39ae355b-9bd5-55ce-b14f-de747087c884" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{(x+h)+(-7x)}{h})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aaa427b3-244e-5498-9b61-671f781cd044" :answer/id #uuid "aaa427b3-244e-5498-9b61-671f781cd044" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{(x+h)+x}{h})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3af6ada-214b-5c5d-8e42-88d836757eb4" :answer/id #uuid "c3af6ada-214b-5c5d-8e42-88d836757eb4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{-7(x+h)-(-7x)}{h})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fee23737-7fe3-58db-a3d3-3228bf0805d8" :answer/id #uuid "fee23737-7fe3-58db-a3d3-3228bf0805d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{-7x-(x+h)}{h})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33e3ce1d-b9dc-5b4f-9df8-3667487f8eec" :answer/id #uuid "33e3ce1d-b9dc-5b4f-9df8-3667487f8eec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{x-7(x+h)}{h})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-779ee099-0fc5-5dca-bd3b-ce3793ba0e8c" :question/id #uuid "779ee099-0fc5-5dca-bd3b-ce3793ba0e8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-47689" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=2x$, the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ can be computed using the definition of the derivative. The first few steps of this calculation are shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2(x+h)-2x}{h} \\\\  & =…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinish the calculation, and hence find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "By proceeding with the remaining calculations we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2(x+h)-2x}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2x+2h-2x}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2h}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(x)=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-11a32983-ce2f-531f-8a50-70b77c16b7ca"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-11a32983-ce2f-531f-8a50-70b77c16b7ca" :answer-field/id #uuid "11a32983-ce2f-531f-8a50-70b77c16b7ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-da196b53-b10f-53b6-a009-921baa312c45" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d603a1ab-9c62-5f2a-a0d7-d9ad36ff8382" "answer-3c63b9a2-0026-5b42-b9d7-d33ef6517d2b" "answer-da196b53-b10f-53b6-a009-921baa312c45" "answer-ae3bfc58-7850-5886-b64b-df0f4824039b" "answer-a025d814-8eca-5444-bebb-105d5442a824"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d603a1ab-9c62-5f2a-a0d7-d9ad36ff8382" :answer/id #uuid "d603a1ab-9c62-5f2a-a0d7-d9ad36ff8382" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c63b9a2-0026-5b42-b9d7-d33ef6517d2b" :answer/id #uuid "3c63b9a2-0026-5b42-b9d7-d33ef6517d2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da196b53-b10f-53b6-a009-921baa312c45" :answer/id #uuid "da196b53-b10f-53b6-a009-921baa312c45" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae3bfc58-7850-5886-b64b-df0f4824039b" :answer/id #uuid "ae3bfc58-7850-5886-b64b-df0f4824039b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a025d814-8eca-5444-bebb-105d5442a824" :answer/id #uuid "a025d814-8eca-5444-bebb-105d5442a824" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a35127e9-e5f3-508e-9d5a-b3279aa78b1a" :question/id #uuid "a35127e9-e5f3-508e-9d5a-b3279aa78b1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-340405" :question/problem "Use the definition of the derivative to find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if  $f(x)=-4{x}^{2}+5x.$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The definition of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=-4{x}^{2}+5x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{-4(x+h)^{2}+5(x+h)-(-4{x}^{2}+5x)}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{-4({x}^{2}+2xh+{h}^{2})+5x+5h+4{x}^{2}-5x}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{-4{x}^{2}-8xh-4{h}^{2}+5x+5h+4{x}^{2}-5x}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{-8xh-4{h}^{2}+5h}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{h(-8x-4h+5)}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(-8x-4h+5) \\\\  & =-8x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a93c79b4-53dc-5d15-9856-ca96c8afc783"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a93c79b4-53dc-5d15-9856-ca96c8afc783" :answer-field/id #uuid "a93c79b4-53dc-5d15-9856-ca96c8afc783" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f05b0eb8-1f64-572b-9eb7-f009dba2ee76" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f05b0eb8-1f64-572b-9eb7-f009dba2ee76"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f05b0eb8-1f64-572b-9eb7-f009dba2ee76" :answer/id #uuid "f05b0eb8-1f64-572b-9eb7-f009dba2ee76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8x+5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088899 :tutorial/content "The instantaneous rate of change of a function $f(x)$ at the point $x=a,$ denoted $f'(a),$ is given by the limit\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{h\\to 0}\\dfrac{f(a+h) - f(a)}{h}.\n$$\n\nThis formula works for a specific point $x=a.$ If we want a method that works for a *general* point $x,$ then we substitute $a=x$ in the formula and get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\lim_{h\\to 0}\\dfrac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f'(x)$ is called the **derivative of $f(x)$ with respect to $x$**. It gives the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)$ at a general point with coordinates $(x,f(x)).$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088900 :tutorial/content "For a given curve $y=f(x),$ the derivative can be represented using several different notations. They are\n\n$$\nf'(x),\\qquad y'(x), \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nIn words, the term $y'(x)$  is said like\n\n- \"$y$ prime of $x$,\" or\n\n- \"$y$ prime,\" or sometimes\n\n- \"$y$ dash.\"\n\nThe term $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ is said like \"dee $y$ by dee $x$.\"\n\nThe important thing to remember is that they all represent the same thing, the derivative." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087898 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133882 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-40abd682-d4fa-55d0-8dea-db00bcc211a6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-40abd682-d4fa-55d0-8dea-db00bcc211a6" :question/id #uuid "40abd682-d4fa-55d0-8dea-db00bcc211a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-109131" :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(1,1)$ and $(0,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+(xy+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/56/5673a3eadfa3c01dbb9e2c8bf497b17f34a834d950621afa7d480af625ce1e18.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=xy+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+(xy+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=y.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a172fe47-3553-5b48-9c9c-d02e78168ac3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a172fe47-3553-5b48-9c9c-d02e78168ac3" :answer-field/id #uuid "a172fe47-3553-5b48-9c9c-d02e78168ac3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3afaeacd-5ea5-5eab-9723-41fa0b9df340" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a17a715e-bff4-5662-80e2-9bdfe2c285ec" "answer-f92e502d-c078-59ac-9d95-4be2a12cda17" "answer-805ee25c-bc46-5a2b-bb0b-a7850c3300e2" "answer-3afaeacd-5ea5-5eab-9723-41fa0b9df340" "answer-143eeb18-acb6-58b9-9d2a-0e8621cf072a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a17a715e-bff4-5662-80e2-9bdfe2c285ec" :answer/id #uuid "a17a715e-bff4-5662-80e2-9bdfe2c285ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f92e502d-c078-59ac-9d95-4be2a12cda17" :answer/id #uuid "f92e502d-c078-59ac-9d95-4be2a12cda17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-805ee25c-bc46-5a2b-bb0b-a7850c3300e2" :answer/id #uuid "805ee25c-bc46-5a2b-bb0b-a7850c3300e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3afaeacd-5ea5-5eab-9723-41fa0b9df340" :answer/id #uuid "3afaeacd-5ea5-5eab-9723-41fa0b9df340" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-143eeb18-acb6-58b9-9d2a-0e8621cf072a" :answer/id #uuid "143eeb18-acb6-58b9-9d2a-0e8621cf072a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186326928 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/6d/6d4f13abad5cc193dccedee3114fbd9cbd9fb8e6b5b3ddb0d07f0ed2ed51f4ff.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le y+1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{y+1}(1+x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186244254 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/23/239867aec8cff7840667d2f4eafa9bc97d4fc5af09b684d863fef06fd8fd28ad.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=2x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{y}+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2{e}^{y}+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):y\\le x\\le 4y-{y}^{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dx+(2x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{e}^{y}+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{y}^{4y-{y}^{2}}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134068 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/2e/2e63a57e50b60d7f0c6a3ce12d164716e2cba71c6bd4c6df10460a35beba4209.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 2x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+2{x}^{2}y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186386397 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/c2/c2e08c233cb31124234ab6e5179998a3bdc148024632c7770bdea46cd37dff83.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx+(xy-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $Q(x,y)=xy-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y+2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x-1\\le y\\le -2x+2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx+(xy-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{-2x+2}y\\text{ }dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186244233 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/5f/5f34ac74599937e0ae181126ff9a3da5b29dfe213614a1f23ca10c7062f22f7d.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(3xy-1)\\,dx+(3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y})\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3xy-1$ and $Q(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 2x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(3xy-1)\\,dx+(3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6x-3x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2x}3x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3x[y]_{{x}^{2}}^{2x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3x(2x-{x}^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{x}^{2}-3{x}^{3})\\,dx \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{3}-\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2(2)^{3}-\\frac{3(2)^{4}}{4} \\\\  & =16-12 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186244244 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/eb/eb0b120cc4d63c8dab18c8acb37f5835e99db746584262f6bb33e8ca99b6a16d.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2xy$ and $Q(x,y)={y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{3x}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}2xy\\,dx+{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(0-2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}/3}^{\\sqrt{3x}}(-2x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2x)[y]_{{x}^{2}/3}^{\\sqrt{3x}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2x)(\\sqrt{3x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})dx \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\sqrt{3}{x}^{3/2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})dx \\\\  & =-2{[\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{3x}}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{12}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-2(\\frac{2(3)^{2}\\sqrt{3(3)}}{5}-\\frac{(3)^{4}}{12}) \\\\  & =-2(\\frac{54}{5}-\\frac{27}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{81}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186244243 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/e7/e788bbcbda4cc715c6989b7de9d08d60e3b0d178eeb9432a342a4c86c4c01b86.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2y-x$ and $Q(x,y)=5x-y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}-x\\le y\\le x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2y-x)\\,dx+(5x-y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5-2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}-x}^{x}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}[y]_{{x}^{2}-x}^{x}\\,dx \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-{x}^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & =3{[{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3(4-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186244245 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/fa/fafd281fc63fb276c9759fd84f722096d494aa3e3fe9441f43aa6ecd1f9dfda9.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(2x+{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2x+{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+2y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2x+{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}/3}(2x-2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[2xy-{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{{x}^{2}/3}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{9})dx \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{45}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3)^{4}}{6}-\\frac{(3)^{5}}{45} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}-\\frac{27}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110700 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b31f5c4b-dccc-5d6d-b26c-76fe9889b868" "question-6181c174-0cfd-5153-a1f3-884abcf7f2a6" "question-a2f709a7-c2aa-55fd-a6a5-4424cd8b1fab" "question-d6cea5e8-bf96-5756-ad7c-875f953f91a2" "question-675df8f0-06ab-563c-85f0-2a4a4ed296f3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110501 :question/problem "The function $f(x) = e^{x-k}+2$ has an inverse function given by $f^{-1}(x) = \\ln{(x-2)}+4.$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{x-k}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{y-k}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{y-k}+2 \\\\ {e}^{y-k} & =x-2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y-k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2) \\\\ y-k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is $f^{-1}(x) = \\ln{(x-2)} + k.$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=4.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110701 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fcede9b9-3aa7-543c-ba0b-831f64ff8671" "question-9d666229-2a75-553e-ab1a-3470df672efe" "question-e0086fad-533f-5e80-a92f-0ac75b17640c" "question-34bef420-975f-5d0f-9f81-67d4bd446432" "question-2340627e-913d-5c02-af0a-4af27f0e0758"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110289 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac12 e^{x+8}-2.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x+8}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{y+8}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{y+8}-2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{y+8} & =x+2 \\\\ {e}^{y+8} & =2x+4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y+8}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4) \\\\ y+8 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4)-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n     \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is $f^{-1}(x) = \\ln{(2x+4)} - 8.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110702 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f4092e37-c259-56c3-8698-e5c1a205391f" "question-bc418213-398e-54af-8583-a0bcd69d268b" "question-9b1ff915-f7df-590e-9573-48667059c317" "question-bf8490ce-55f6-59c1-8771-a458611eb22b" "question-6267302e-96c5-5885-be5f-0252c2b87de1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110500 :question/problem "What is the inverse of $f(x) =\\ln(x+3)+6?$" :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny = \\ln(x+3)+6\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+3)+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+3)+6 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+3) & =x-6 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+3)} & ={e}^{x-6} \\\\ y+3 & ={e}^{x-6} \\\\ y & ={e}^{x-6}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is $f^{-1}(x) = e^{x-6}-3.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110703 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-199584af-715c-57d8-b77d-cfea8258f652" "question-02054798-e30b-5bba-b50a-1ea23b8ac8ca" "question-04376890-0a44-56a8-b428-8110db81b255" "question-3b5ffb6f-7067-559d-aec0-81e9ef0a06b7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110502 :question/problem "What is the inverse of $f(x) = 2\\log_4(3x)-1?$" :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2{log}_{4}⁡(3x)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{log}_{4}⁡(3y)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{log}_{4}⁡(3y)-1 \\\\ 2{log}_{4}⁡(3y) & =x+1 \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡(3y) & =\\frac{x+1}{2} \\\\ {4}^{{log}_{4}⁡(3y)} & ={4}^{(x+1)/2} \\\\ 3y & ={4}^{(x+1)/2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}{4}^{(x+1)/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is $f^{-1}(x) =\\dfrac13 4^{(x+1)/2}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-b31f5c4b-dccc-5d6d-b26c-76fe9889b868" :question/id #uuid "b31f5c4b-dccc-5d6d-b26c-76fe9889b868" :question/math-academy-id "q-250404" :question/problem "The inverse of the function $f(x)={e}^{2-x}$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{2-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{2-y} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2-y}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =2-y \\\\ y & =2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-71520f5c-b440-5a14-bf95-c16d81c6f6d1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-71520f5c-b440-5a14-bf95-c16d81c6f6d1" :answer-field/id #uuid "71520f5c-b440-5a14-bf95-c16d81c6f6d1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d19974ec-20b9-5d83-8c72-b2d36b8377e4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d19974ec-20b9-5d83-8c72-b2d36b8377e4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d19974ec-20b9-5d83-8c72-b2d36b8377e4" :answer/id #uuid "d19974ec-20b9-5d83-8c72-b2d36b8377e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2-\\ln(x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6181c174-0cfd-5153-a1f3-884abcf7f2a6" :question/id #uuid "6181c174-0cfd-5153-a1f3-884abcf7f2a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-148278" :question/problem "The function $f(x)={e}^{x+k}+3$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)+5.$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{x+k}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{y+k}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{y+k}+3 \\\\ {e}^{y+k} & =x-3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y+k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3) \\\\ y+k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)-k.$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=-5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b91ce61b-a6cb-5ce7-8bdc-e10fbcf3ddfa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b91ce61b-a6cb-5ce7-8bdc-e10fbcf3ddfa" :answer-field/id #uuid "b91ce61b-a6cb-5ce7-8bdc-e10fbcf3ddfa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b0712310-2288-594f-a713-0325d1c58202" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b0712310-2288-594f-a713-0325d1c58202" "answer-beb19ed9-fffa-5b00-9533-3a872c9ee6ce" "answer-500968d9-0b02-594f-9796-38a6d0857002" "answer-ce4ef968-3737-5e23-9278-d9b7973ad60c" "answer-64a8ae10-ee67-50d8-90ac-7e8244b9414e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0712310-2288-594f-a713-0325d1c58202" :answer/id #uuid "b0712310-2288-594f-a713-0325d1c58202" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-beb19ed9-fffa-5b00-9533-3a872c9ee6ce" :answer/id #uuid "beb19ed9-fffa-5b00-9533-3a872c9ee6ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-500968d9-0b02-594f-9796-38a6d0857002" :answer/id #uuid "500968d9-0b02-594f-9796-38a6d0857002" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce4ef968-3737-5e23-9278-d9b7973ad60c" :answer/id #uuid "ce4ef968-3737-5e23-9278-d9b7973ad60c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-64a8ae10-ee67-50d8-90ac-7e8244b9414e" :answer/id #uuid "64a8ae10-ee67-50d8-90ac-7e8244b9414e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a2f709a7-c2aa-55fd-a6a5-4424cd8b1fab" :question/id #uuid "a2f709a7-c2aa-55fd-a6a5-4424cd8b1fab" :question/math-academy-id "q-148313" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)={7}^{x-2}+4.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={7}^{x-2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={7}^{y-2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={7}^{y-2}+4 \\\\ x-4 & ={7}^{y-2} \\\\ {log}_{7}⁡(x-4) & ={log}_{7}⁡({7}^{y-2}) \\\\ {log}_{7}⁡(x-4) & =y-2 \\\\ y & ={log}_{7}⁡(x-4)+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{7}⁡(x-4)+2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-90658ef9-d513-512b-9c60-b9724abb9088"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-90658ef9-d513-512b-9c60-b9724abb9088" :answer-field/id #uuid "90658ef9-d513-512b-9c60-b9724abb9088" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-46f4273e-643e-5140-9bb3-37930495687c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d99198fe-8160-5db2-9ef6-8b317c7d5096" "answer-c58fc5b6-7692-51a1-abef-c6e7a022b6d8" "answer-e1e17c2b-0b45-5f50-a9cb-32c3aec63676" "answer-2ee8abd7-c8b0-5395-b1d2-db97d2fbe877" "answer-46f4273e-643e-5140-9bb3-37930495687c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d99198fe-8160-5db2-9ef6-8b317c7d5096" :answer/id #uuid "d99198fe-8160-5db2-9ef6-8b317c7d5096" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{log}_{7}⁡(x)}{4}+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c58fc5b6-7692-51a1-abef-c6e7a022b6d8" :answer/id #uuid "c58fc5b6-7692-51a1-abef-c6e7a022b6d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{log}_{7}⁡(x-4)}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1e17c2b-0b45-5f50-a9cb-32c3aec63676" :answer/id #uuid "e1e17c2b-0b45-5f50-a9cb-32c3aec63676" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{7}⁡(x-2)+4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ee8abd7-c8b0-5395-b1d2-db97d2fbe877" :answer/id #uuid "2ee8abd7-c8b0-5395-b1d2-db97d2fbe877" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{7}⁡(x-2)-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46f4273e-643e-5140-9bb3-37930495687c" :answer/id #uuid "46f4273e-643e-5140-9bb3-37930495687c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{7}⁡(x-4)+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d6cea5e8-bf96-5756-ad7c-875f953f91a2" :question/id #uuid "d6cea5e8-bf96-5756-ad7c-875f953f91a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-250405" :question/problem "The inverse of the function $f(x)=-{3}^{4x}$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{3}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-{3}^{4y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-{3}^{4y} \\\\ -x & ={3}^{4y} \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(-x) & ={log}_{3}⁡({3}^{4y}) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(-x) & =4y \\\\ 4y & ={log}_{3}⁡(-x) \\\\ y & =\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(-x)}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(-x)}{4}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1415ab41-137c-58c7-97a0-9e85dd2a2d52"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1415ab41-137c-58c7-97a0-9e85dd2a2d52" :answer-field/id #uuid "1415ab41-137c-58c7-97a0-9e85dd2a2d52" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-82dfab9f-2880-5450-8652-1147b1151f26" :answer-field/choices ["answer-82dfab9f-2880-5450-8652-1147b1151f26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82dfab9f-2880-5450-8652-1147b1151f26" :answer/id #uuid "82dfab9f-2880-5450-8652-1147b1151f26" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\log_3(-x)}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-675df8f0-06ab-563c-85f0-2a4a4ed296f3" :question/id #uuid "675df8f0-06ab-563c-85f0-2a4a4ed296f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-250406" :question/problem "The inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{8}^{6x}}{3}$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{8}^{6x}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{8}^{6y}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{8}^{6y}}{3} \\\\ 3x & ={8}^{6y} \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡(3x) & ={log}_{8}⁡({8}^{6y}) \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡(3x) & =6y \\\\ 6y & ={log}_{8}⁡(3x) \\\\ y & =\\frac{{log}_{8}⁡(3x)}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{log}_{8}⁡(3x)}{6}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ff656e20-f3df-5084-9cd4-4ad94db5cfe5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ff656e20-f3df-5084-9cd4-4ad94db5cfe5" :answer-field/id #uuid "ff656e20-f3df-5084-9cd4-4ad94db5cfe5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d41088e7-fcca-5795-a840-68f0d4b1a3c8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d41088e7-fcca-5795-a840-68f0d4b1a3c8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d41088e7-fcca-5795-a840-68f0d4b1a3c8" :answer/id #uuid "d41088e7-fcca-5795-a840-68f0d4b1a3c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\log_8(3x)}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fcede9b9-3aa7-543c-ba0b-831f64ff8671" :question/id #uuid "fcede9b9-3aa7-543c-ba0b-831f64ff8671" :question/math-academy-id "q-148370" :question/problem "The function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{kx}+2$ has an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5).\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{kx}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{ky}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{ky}+2 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{ky} & =x-2 \\\\ {3}^{ky} & =\\frac{5x-10}{2} \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡({3}^{ky}) & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5) \\\\ ky & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{k}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{k}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5).$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5ce6d141-0aa1-5d3d-a0d1-784268e63420"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5ce6d141-0aa1-5d3d-a0d1-784268e63420" :answer-field/id #uuid "5ce6d141-0aa1-5d3d-a0d1-784268e63420" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-56203f17-53a8-5839-bf69-62f170218fff" :answer-field/choices ["answer-036eaa53-7479-5120-8bab-05396fca46ed" "answer-be5dfe54-d2c6-51bc-ba60-5b77415cb27f" "answer-c7815962-506a-58a7-a401-291b446f0170" "answer-56203f17-53a8-5839-bf69-62f170218fff" "answer-ead0b0b9-c829-5ec7-932a-5da8c8741b26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-036eaa53-7479-5120-8bab-05396fca46ed" :answer/id #uuid "036eaa53-7479-5120-8bab-05396fca46ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be5dfe54-d2c6-51bc-ba60-5b77415cb27f" :answer/id #uuid "be5dfe54-d2c6-51bc-ba60-5b77415cb27f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7815962-506a-58a7-a401-291b446f0170" :answer/id #uuid "c7815962-506a-58a7-a401-291b446f0170" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56203f17-53a8-5839-bf69-62f170218fff" :answer/id #uuid "56203f17-53a8-5839-bf69-62f170218fff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ead0b0b9-c829-5ec7-932a-5da8c8741b26" :answer/id #uuid "ead0b0b9-c829-5ec7-932a-5da8c8741b26" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9d666229-2a75-553e-ab1a-3470df672efe" :question/id #uuid "9d666229-2a75-553e-ab1a-3470df672efe" :question/math-academy-id "q-250479" :question/problem "If the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{k}\\cdot {10}^{1+x/2}+1$ has an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=2\\operatorname{log}⁡(1-x)-2,\n$$\n\n \nwhat is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{k}\\cdot {10}^{1+x/2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{k}\\cdot {10}^{1+y/2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{k}\\cdot {10}^{1+y/2}+1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{k}\\cdot {10}^{1+y/2} & =x-1 \\\\ {10}^{1+y/2} & =kx-k \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{1+y/2}) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(kx-k) \\\\ 1+\\frac{y}{2} & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(kx-k) \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(kx-k)-1 \\\\ y & =2\\operatorname{log}⁡(kx-k)-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=2\\operatorname{log}⁡(kx-k)-2.$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7dea4e2d-cd83-5ced-88cf-1d95740dcf47"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7dea4e2d-cd83-5ced-88cf-1d95740dcf47" :answer-field/id #uuid "7dea4e2d-cd83-5ced-88cf-1d95740dcf47" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-750c0d00-afda-5067-a770-e6e2fc508277" :answer-field/choices ["answer-750c0d00-afda-5067-a770-e6e2fc508277"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-750c0d00-afda-5067-a770-e6e2fc508277" :answer/id #uuid "750c0d00-afda-5067-a770-e6e2fc508277" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e0086fad-533f-5e80-a92f-0ac75b17640c" :question/id #uuid "e0086fad-533f-5e80-a92f-0ac75b17640c" :question/math-academy-id "q-148359" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2y}-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2y}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2y} & =x+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {3}^{2y} & =4x+2 \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡({3}^{2y}) & ={log}_{3}⁡(4x+2) \\\\ 2y & ={log}_{3}⁡(4x+2) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(4x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(4x+2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-52664e87-7542-5e32-b8c8-3ec5d80cbfd4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-52664e87-7542-5e32-b8c8-3ec5d80cbfd4" :answer-field/id #uuid "52664e87-7542-5e32-b8c8-3ec5d80cbfd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e2312f9d-2baf-5e9d-93dd-0654789452bd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b95fcc5a-b97c-570a-b6aa-9be9f9c37ee4" "answer-1d1902df-1e1a-57b7-92b9-f031a1d54cb1" "answer-e2312f9d-2baf-5e9d-93dd-0654789452bd" "answer-b867ae05-4768-52a1-a7ab-9ac578fa106a" "answer-e2ef0cbc-d0f2-5d87-96ee-056aed052a7a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b95fcc5a-b97c-570a-b6aa-9be9f9c37ee4" :answer/id #uuid "b95fcc5a-b97c-570a-b6aa-9be9f9c37ee4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=2{log}_{3}⁡(4x+\\frac{1}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d1902df-1e1a-57b7-92b9-f031a1d54cb1" :answer/id #uuid "1d1902df-1e1a-57b7-92b9-f031a1d54cb1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=2{log}_{3}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e2312f9d-2baf-5e9d-93dd-0654789452bd" :answer/id #uuid "e2312f9d-2baf-5e9d-93dd-0654789452bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(4x+2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b867ae05-4768-52a1-a7ab-9ac578fa106a" :answer/id #uuid "b867ae05-4768-52a1-a7ab-9ac578fa106a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{3}⁡(4x+\\frac{1}{2})+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e2ef0cbc-d0f2-5d87-96ee-056aed052a7a" :answer/id #uuid "e2ef0cbc-d0f2-5d87-96ee-056aed052a7a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{3}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{2})+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-34bef420-975f-5d0f-9f81-67d4bd446432" :question/id #uuid "34bef420-975f-5d0f-9f81-67d4bd446432" :question/math-academy-id "q-148349" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{x+1}-2.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{x+1}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{y+1}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{y+1}-2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{y+1} & =x+2 \\\\ {e}^{y+1} & =2x+4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y+1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4) \\\\ y+1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4)-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-03b4b32e-b15d-5051-934e-0831b4ef34cf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-03b4b32e-b15d-5051-934e-0831b4ef34cf" :answer-field/id #uuid "03b4b32e-b15d-5051-934e-0831b4ef34cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f4779bf5-8fa6-5176-af16-a00529d6cf4b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f4779bf5-8fa6-5176-af16-a00529d6cf4b" "answer-7045ffc3-7fd2-5590-80b0-9f2bcb29d8e6" "answer-5fdb24cb-9d60-58f8-b13e-d7ee93dff0bd" "answer-2af70751-5a25-5bbe-a87a-5793529a42b7" "answer-45173f49-dca6-5562-aa6b-a16c19825b58"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f4779bf5-8fa6-5176-af16-a00529d6cf4b" :answer/id #uuid "f4779bf5-8fa6-5176-af16-a00529d6cf4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4)-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7045ffc3-7fd2-5590-80b0-9f2bcb29d8e6" :answer/id #uuid "7045ffc3-7fd2-5590-80b0-9f2bcb29d8e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-1)-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5fdb24cb-9d60-58f8-b13e-d7ee93dff0bd" :answer/id #uuid "5fdb24cb-9d60-58f8-b13e-d7ee93dff0bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x+1)+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2af70751-5a25-5bbe-a87a-5793529a42b7" :answer/id #uuid "2af70751-5a25-5bbe-a87a-5793529a42b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x+2)+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45173f49-dca6-5562-aa6b-a16c19825b58" :answer/id #uuid "45173f49-dca6-5562-aa6b-a16c19825b58" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2340627e-913d-5c02-af0a-4af27f0e0758" :question/id #uuid "2340627e-913d-5c02-af0a-4af27f0e0758" :question/math-academy-id "q-148367" :question/problem "The function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2x-k}$ has an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3}).\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2x-k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2y-k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2y-k} \\\\ {5}^{2y-k} & =\\frac{2}{3}-x \\\\ {5}^{2y-k} & =\\frac{2-3x}{3} \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡({5}^{2y-k}) & ={log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3}) \\\\ 2y-k & ={log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3}) \\\\ 2y & ={log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3})+k \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3})+\\frac{k}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3})+\\frac{k}{2}$  \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7dd89537-63c4-5254-bd6d-b7533a181887"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7dd89537-63c4-5254-bd6d-b7533a181887" :answer-field/id #uuid "7dd89537-63c4-5254-bd6d-b7533a181887" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-173a6cfb-d807-5412-8e19-23721709e4c9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-173a6cfb-d807-5412-8e19-23721709e4c9" "answer-f9d7fe5d-6a2b-5e6d-aa5b-be15665a7768" "answer-60e2afc2-23df-527e-8d4d-06bcc3f5634f" "answer-11623763-75f4-582c-8cf1-89865dc45e28" "answer-f8d10f1c-8d35-5c14-a45c-90112e2423c6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-173a6cfb-d807-5412-8e19-23721709e4c9" :answer/id #uuid "173a6cfb-d807-5412-8e19-23721709e4c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f9d7fe5d-6a2b-5e6d-aa5b-be15665a7768" :answer/id #uuid "f9d7fe5d-6a2b-5e6d-aa5b-be15665a7768" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60e2afc2-23df-527e-8d4d-06bcc3f5634f" :answer/id #uuid "60e2afc2-23df-527e-8d4d-06bcc3f5634f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11623763-75f4-582c-8cf1-89865dc45e28" :answer/id #uuid "11623763-75f4-582c-8cf1-89865dc45e28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f8d10f1c-8d35-5c14-a45c-90112e2423c6" :answer/id #uuid "f8d10f1c-8d35-5c14-a45c-90112e2423c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f4092e37-c259-56c3-8698-e5c1a205391f" :question/id #uuid "f4092e37-c259-56c3-8698-e5c1a205391f" :question/math-academy-id "q-250483" :question/problem "The inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)+\\frac{1}{2}$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(y)+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:        \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(y)+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡y \\\\ 2x-1 & ={log}_{2}⁡y \\\\ {2}^{2x-1} & ={2}^{{log}_{2}⁡y} \\\\ {2}^{2x-1} & =y \\\\ y & ={2}^{2x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)={2}^{2x-1}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-94613fd4-07f9-5587-8c52-2ee0c031ef37"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-94613fd4-07f9-5587-8c52-2ee0c031ef37" :answer-field/id #uuid "94613fd4-07f9-5587-8c52-2ee0c031ef37" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ef6e90d4-7f02-516b-ac2e-50a86e66d6fb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ef6e90d4-7f02-516b-ac2e-50a86e66d6fb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef6e90d4-7f02-516b-ac2e-50a86e66d6fb" :answer/id #uuid "ef6e90d4-7f02-516b-ac2e-50a86e66d6fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2^{2x-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bc418213-398e-54af-8583-a0bcd69d268b" :question/id #uuid "bc418213-398e-54af-8583-a0bcd69d268b" :question/math-academy-id "q-46622" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)={log}_{2}⁡(x)+8.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={log}_{2}⁡(x)+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{2}⁡(y)+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equations, as follows:        \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{2}⁡(y)+8 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(y) & =x-8 \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(y)} & ={2}^{x-8} \\\\ y & ={2}^{x-8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)={2}^{x-8}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a8174fdc-5c9a-5c07-a9f4-f2a0413bcb78"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a8174fdc-5c9a-5c07-a9f4-f2a0413bcb78" :answer-field/id #uuid "a8174fdc-5c9a-5c07-a9f4-f2a0413bcb78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-40d40771-4eff-5689-a13b-0ce325f7611e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7d88a917-1a9a-5eac-a57a-00685fc9b5ba" "answer-c58c52c9-7d88-54cc-b33c-6e8f43208112" "answer-40d40771-4eff-5689-a13b-0ce325f7611e" "answer-d73af309-c34a-5c48-b101-ba26f74e0435" "answer-196682b1-bbf3-576e-ba6f-a649f7fc16ca"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d88a917-1a9a-5eac-a57a-00685fc9b5ba" :answer/id #uuid "7d88a917-1a9a-5eac-a57a-00685fc9b5ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={2}^{8x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c58c52c9-7d88-54cc-b33c-6e8f43208112" :answer/id #uuid "c58c52c9-7d88-54cc-b33c-6e8f43208112" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={2}^{x+8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40d40771-4eff-5689-a13b-0ce325f7611e" :answer/id #uuid "40d40771-4eff-5689-a13b-0ce325f7611e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={2}^{x-8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d73af309-c34a-5c48-b101-ba26f74e0435" :answer/id #uuid "d73af309-c34a-5c48-b101-ba26f74e0435" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={2}^{x}+8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-196682b1-bbf3-576e-ba6f-a649f7fc16ca" :answer/id #uuid "196682b1-bbf3-576e-ba6f-a649f7fc16ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={2}^{x}-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9b1ff915-f7df-590e-9573-48667059c317" :question/id #uuid "9b1ff915-f7df-590e-9573-48667059c317" :question/math-academy-id "q-46621" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{y}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{y}{3}) \\\\ {10}^{x} & ={10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(y/3)} \\\\ {10}^{x} & =\\frac{y}{3} \\\\ y & =3\\cdot {10}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=3\\cdot {10}^{x}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1ff5f77d-c752-52bb-b6ec-a317055ea25e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1ff5f77d-c752-52bb-b6ec-a317055ea25e" :answer-field/id #uuid "1ff5f77d-c752-52bb-b6ec-a317055ea25e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a68559e7-d056-5e43-81d2-70700c2a99e9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a68559e7-d056-5e43-81d2-70700c2a99e9" "answer-20e1836d-75eb-5de8-9c9c-af521731af67" "answer-cdffd6c4-c3d8-5c8f-9f1a-ca2d437d5ed7" "answer-338596eb-3c1b-5bb8-a994-1d2cbd79a6de" "answer-b4ad9448-aed0-5988-be94-5a5e58fa5d2f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a68559e7-d056-5e43-81d2-70700c2a99e9" :answer/id #uuid "a68559e7-d056-5e43-81d2-70700c2a99e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=3\\cdot {10}^{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20e1836d-75eb-5de8-9c9c-af521731af67" :answer/id #uuid "20e1836d-75eb-5de8-9c9c-af521731af67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{10}^{x}}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cdffd6c4-c3d8-5c8f-9f1a-ca2d437d5ed7" :answer/id #uuid "cdffd6c4-c3d8-5c8f-9f1a-ca2d437d5ed7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={10}^{3x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-338596eb-3c1b-5bb8-a994-1d2cbd79a6de" :answer/id #uuid "338596eb-3c1b-5bb8-a994-1d2cbd79a6de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={10}^{x/3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4ad9448-aed0-5988-be94-5a5e58fa5d2f" :answer/id #uuid "b4ad9448-aed0-5988-be94-5a5e58fa5d2f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={10}^{x}+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bf8490ce-55f6-59c1-8771-a458611eb22b" :question/id #uuid "bf8490ce-55f6-59c1-8771-a458611eb22b" :question/math-academy-id "q-46629" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7x).$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:        \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7y) \\\\ {e}^{x} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7y)} \\\\ {e}^{x} & =7y \\\\ y & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{7}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c659a6ca-0f92-5137-b8eb-7f163fce4f16"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c659a6ca-0f92-5137-b8eb-7f163fce4f16" :answer-field/id #uuid "c659a6ca-0f92-5137-b8eb-7f163fce4f16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-acc3787f-1577-51a3-8e8c-ba211e4d0309" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4924d51b-e261-507e-adfc-8135105e1bc1" "answer-acc3787f-1577-51a3-8e8c-ba211e4d0309" "answer-a9d3cc1a-fcfb-5bbd-b5a0-9fde202d0224" "answer-448a3c24-8cfd-569c-b0a3-1c765de60d34" "answer-5fd2814c-01a6-54bb-ace6-f4371712810f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4924d51b-e261-507e-adfc-8135105e1bc1" :answer/id #uuid "4924d51b-e261-507e-adfc-8135105e1bc1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=7{e}^{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-acc3787f-1577-51a3-8e8c-ba211e4d0309" :answer/id #uuid "acc3787f-1577-51a3-8e8c-ba211e4d0309" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9d3cc1a-fcfb-5bbd-b5a0-9fde202d0224" :answer/id #uuid "a9d3cc1a-fcfb-5bbd-b5a0-9fde202d0224" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={e}^{7x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-448a3c24-8cfd-569c-b0a3-1c765de60d34" :answer/id #uuid "448a3c24-8cfd-569c-b0a3-1c765de60d34" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={e}^{x/7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5fd2814c-01a6-54bb-ace6-f4371712810f" :answer/id #uuid "5fd2814c-01a6-54bb-ace6-f4371712810f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={e}^{x}+7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6267302e-96c5-5885-be5f-0252c2b87de1" :question/id #uuid "6267302e-96c5-5885-be5f-0252c2b87de1" :question/math-academy-id "q-81645" :question/problem "The function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x-k)$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}-9}{3}.$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x-k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3y-k).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3y-k) \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3y-k)} & ={e}^{x} \\\\ 3y-k & ={e}^{x} \\\\ 3y & ={e}^{x}+k \\\\ y & =\\frac{{e}^{x}+k}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}+k}{3}.$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=-9.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a19c6a94-00d3-5ae9-abde-806f2ebd3f92"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a19c6a94-00d3-5ae9-abde-806f2ebd3f92" :answer-field/id #uuid "a19c6a94-00d3-5ae9-abde-806f2ebd3f92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3d74ec5f-ab18-5937-82d1-2c8a347321a5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-08846a6c-13c1-5b18-885e-0b1adeacc3f0" "answer-395537a6-7a30-5d62-853c-cb1f3b891411" "answer-3d74ec5f-ab18-5937-82d1-2c8a347321a5" "answer-23b61276-d5bf-535a-a27d-e9a54b1b9dbf" "answer-54b483d6-0bec-5ca5-9f74-c3fcf0ede81f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-08846a6c-13c1-5b18-885e-0b1adeacc3f0" :answer/id #uuid "08846a6c-13c1-5b18-885e-0b1adeacc3f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-395537a6-7a30-5d62-853c-cb1f3b891411" :answer/id #uuid "395537a6-7a30-5d62-853c-cb1f3b891411" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d74ec5f-ab18-5937-82d1-2c8a347321a5" :answer/id #uuid "3d74ec5f-ab18-5937-82d1-2c8a347321a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23b61276-d5bf-535a-a27d-e9a54b1b9dbf" :answer/id #uuid "23b61276-d5bf-535a-a27d-e9a54b1b9dbf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54b483d6-0bec-5ca5-9f74-c3fcf0ede81f" :answer/id #uuid "54b483d6-0bec-5ca5-9f74-c3fcf0ede81f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-199584af-715c-57d8-b77d-cfea8258f652" :question/id #uuid "199584af-715c-57d8-b77d-cfea8258f652" :question/math-academy-id "q-250510" :question/problem "If the function $f(x)=3{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{x-1}{3})-k?$ has an inverse function given by\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=3\\cdot {2}^{(x+6)/3}+1,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=3{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{x-1}{3})-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{3})-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{3})-k \\\\ 3{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{3}) & =x+k \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{3}) & =\\frac{x+k}{3} \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡((y-1)/3)} & ={2}^{(x+k)/3} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{3} & ={2}^{(x+k)/3} \\\\ y-1 & =3\\cdot {2}^{(x+k)/3} \\\\ y & =3\\cdot {2}^{(x+k)/3}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=3\\cdot {2}^{(x+k)/3}+1.$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-08ffe6b8-fc77-5a91-a8ee-c6dda37b1ac2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-08ffe6b8-fc77-5a91-a8ee-c6dda37b1ac2" :answer-field/id #uuid "08ffe6b8-fc77-5a91-a8ee-c6dda37b1ac2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2500f223-2c89-5c0f-a8ea-dd1a400d8458" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2500f223-2c89-5c0f-a8ea-dd1a400d8458"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2500f223-2c89-5c0f-a8ea-dd1a400d8458" :answer/id #uuid "2500f223-2c89-5c0f-a8ea-dd1a400d8458" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-02054798-e30b-5bba-b50a-1ea23b8ac8ca" :question/id #uuid "02054798-e30b-5bba-b50a-1ea23b8ac8ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-90426" :question/problem "The function $f(x)={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x-1}{k})$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{3}^{x+2}}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}.$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x-1}{k})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5y-1}{k}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5y-1}{k}) \\\\ {3}^{{log}_{3}⁡((5y-1)/k)} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{5y-1}{k} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ 5y-1 & ={3}^{x}\\cdot k \\\\ 5y & ={3}^{x}\\cdot k+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{{3}^{x}\\cdot k}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{3}^{x}\\cdot k}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}.$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k={3}^{2}=9.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f2a45213-b6de-5733-ae9b-b7816fa719f4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f2a45213-b6de-5733-ae9b-b7816fa719f4" :answer-field/id #uuid "f2a45213-b6de-5733-ae9b-b7816fa719f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-84da75f0-b8f7-5316-851b-9650d5ba1bd4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d5a9352d-3699-501f-8b2e-6e6f26adfaa5" "answer-7577d28a-3de8-5ca6-81d4-c07e03712aff" "answer-161bbede-61ba-5590-8f41-6ce72d807195" "answer-61a9fdab-c6b1-5177-a8d3-66e8ab9d8e73" "answer-84da75f0-b8f7-5316-851b-9650d5ba1bd4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d5a9352d-3699-501f-8b2e-6e6f26adfaa5" :answer/id #uuid "d5a9352d-3699-501f-8b2e-6e6f26adfaa5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7577d28a-3de8-5ca6-81d4-c07e03712aff" :answer/id #uuid "7577d28a-3de8-5ca6-81d4-c07e03712aff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-161bbede-61ba-5590-8f41-6ce72d807195" :answer/id #uuid "161bbede-61ba-5590-8f41-6ce72d807195" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-61a9fdab-c6b1-5177-a8d3-66e8ab9d8e73" :answer/id #uuid "61a9fdab-c6b1-5177-a8d3-66e8ab9d8e73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-84da75f0-b8f7-5316-851b-9650d5ba1bd4" :answer/id #uuid "84da75f0-b8f7-5316-851b-9650d5ba1bd4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110985 :question/problem "The inverse of the function $f(x)=5{log}_{4}⁡(10x-1)$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5{log}_{4}⁡(10x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5{log}_{4}⁡(10y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5{log}_{4}⁡(10y-1) \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡(10y-1) & =\\frac{x}{5} \\\\ {4}^{{log}_{4}⁡(10y-1)} & ={4}^{x/5} \\\\ 10y-1 & ={4}^{x/5} \\\\ 10y & ={4}^{x/5}+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{{4}^{x/5}+1}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{4}^{x/5}+1}{10}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107628 :answer-field/correct "answer-d3dc6496-77c0-520f-b529-b7f00becd583" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d3dc6496-77c0-520f-b529-b7f00becd583"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3dc6496-77c0-520f-b529-b7f00becd583" :answer/id #uuid "d3dc6496-77c0-520f-b529-b7f00becd583" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4^{\\frac{x}{5}}+1}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-04376890-0a44-56a8-b428-8110db81b255" :question/id #uuid "04376890-0a44-56a8-b428-8110db81b255" :question/math-academy-id "q-250505" :question/problem "The inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1 \\\\ x-1 & =\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ 4x-4 & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) & =4x-4 \\\\ {3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(y/4-1/2)} & ={3}^{4x-4} \\\\ \\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2} & ={3}^{4x-4} \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & ={3}^{4x-4}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =4({3}^{4x-4}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ y & =4({3}^{4x-4})+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=4({3}^{4x-4})+2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d2d74a14-bc3d-5d29-8730-61d8c4d650f9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d2d74a14-bc3d-5d29-8730-61d8c4d650f9" :answer-field/id #uuid "d2d74a14-bc3d-5d29-8730-61d8c4d650f9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4655caa9-d5c5-51c5-8bd4-8475855ddad8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4655caa9-d5c5-51c5-8bd4-8475855ddad8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4655caa9-d5c5-51c5-8bd4-8475855ddad8" :answer/id #uuid "4655caa9-d5c5-51c5-8bd4-8475855ddad8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4({3}^{4x-4})+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3b5ffb6f-7067-559d-aec0-81e9ef0a06b7" :question/id #uuid "3b5ffb6f-7067-559d-aec0-81e9ef0a06b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-250507" :question/problem "If the function $f(x)={log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{x-1}{5})-k$ has an inverse function given by\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=5\\cdot {2}^{x-3}+1,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{x-1}{5})-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{5})-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{5})-k \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{5}) & =x+k \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡((y-1)/5)} & ={2}^{x+k} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{5} & ={2}^{x+k} \\\\ y-1 & =5\\cdot {2}^{x+k} \\\\ y & =5\\cdot {2}^{x+k}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=5\\cdot {2}^{x+k}+1.$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=-3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-075d7fee-b2d5-5957-a28d-a7eec528b93a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-075d7fee-b2d5-5957-a28d-a7eec528b93a" :answer-field/id #uuid "075d7fee-b2d5-5957-a28d-a7eec528b93a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f374302c-dbd0-5652-afd7-79c66b75d75d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f374302c-dbd0-5652-afd7-79c66b75d75d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f374302c-dbd0-5652-afd7-79c66b75d75d" :answer/id #uuid "f374302c-dbd0-5652-afd7-79c66b75d75d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111905 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the transformed exponential function \n\n$$\nf(x)=2^{5x}+1\n$$\n\nand we wish to find the inverse function $f^{-1}(x).$ We can do this using the following three steps.\n\n**Step 1:** We start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={2}^{5x}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Then, we interchange $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={2}^{5y}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={2}^{5y}+1 \\\\ x-1 & ={2}^{5y} \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(x-1) & ={log}_{2}⁡({2}^{5y}) \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(x-1) & =5y \\\\ 5y & ={log}_{2}⁡(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{5}{log}_{2}⁡(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\log_2{(x-1)}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111881 :tutorial/content "Now suppose we wish to find the inverse of the logarithmic function \n\n$$\nf(x)=3\\log_{2}(x-1)+1.\n$$\n\nWe can find $f^{-1}(x)$ using the same three steps as before.\n\n**Step 1:** We start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = 3\\log_{2}(x-1)+1\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Then, we interchange $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx = 3\\log_{2}(y-1)+1\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{log}_{2}⁡(y-1)+1 & =x \\\\ 3{log}_{2}⁡(y-1) & =x-1 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(y-1) & =\\frac{x-1}{3} \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(y-1)} & ={2}^{(x-1)/3} \\\\ y-1 & ={2}^{(x-1)/3} \\\\ y & ={2}^{(x-1)/3}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = {2}^{(x-1)/3}+1.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110841 :activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110607 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c0394c3f-264a-5afa-ba91-982051eae5c0" "question-062c010f-c635-5129-83e7-3b14e937de12" "question-e999e0ab-2ba5-58ed-8052-a9586c979824"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c0394c3f-264a-5afa-ba91-982051eae5c0" :question/id #uuid "c0394c3f-264a-5afa-ba91-982051eae5c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-19176" :question/problem "Find the exact solution to $5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)=15.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x) & =15 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x) & =\\frac{15}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x) & =3 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)} & ={e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}=2x.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={e}^{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e95ab9e0-e927-517c-a06b-b2eb56a8f8a5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e95ab9e0-e927-517c-a06b-b2eb56a8f8a5" :answer-field/id #uuid "e95ab9e0-e927-517c-a06b-b2eb56a8f8a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4819ad5b-9efa-59de-9c5c-40a63dadea1b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-eda2fd74-1804-5bf0-a2e8-9ade861ff1b1" "answer-485ce7f6-d535-5e25-8131-84e8b304bdd0" "answer-d13a84a6-bc8d-5087-a25f-d140018a6241" "answer-4819ad5b-9efa-59de-9c5c-40a63dadea1b" "answer-819a7a90-d083-5d62-b2d3-fb97bead1c45"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eda2fd74-1804-5bf0-a2e8-9ade861ff1b1" :answer/id #uuid "eda2fd74-1804-5bf0-a2e8-9ade861ff1b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\frac{15-{e}^{5}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-485ce7f6-d535-5e25-8131-84e8b304bdd0" :answer/id #uuid "485ce7f6-d535-5e25-8131-84e8b304bdd0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\frac{2-{e}^{5}}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d13a84a6-bc8d-5087-a25f-d140018a6241" :answer/id #uuid "d13a84a6-bc8d-5087-a25f-d140018a6241" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\frac{{e}^{15}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4819ad5b-9efa-59de-9c5c-40a63dadea1b" :answer/id #uuid "4819ad5b-9efa-59de-9c5c-40a63dadea1b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\frac{{e}^{3}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-819a7a90-d083-5d62-b2d3-fb97bead1c45" :answer/id #uuid "819a7a90-d083-5d62-b2d3-fb97bead1c45" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\frac{{e}^{5}}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110608 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9bdc2bf5-cb58-518d-9b62-85ffcb2b1edc" "question-b08ed48d-cd63-5608-bd95-a1073f0e3d2b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-9bdc2bf5-cb58-518d-9b62-85ffcb2b1edc" :question/id #uuid "9bdc2bf5-cb58-518d-9b62-85ffcb2b1edc" :question/math-academy-id "q-70040" :question/problem "If $2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=20$, then what is the value of  $x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify our equation by collecting the like logarithmic terms, and isolating the logarithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =20 \\\\ (2+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =20 \\\\ 5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =20 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =\\frac{20}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =4 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} & ={e}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=x.$ \n\nTherefore, $x={e}^{4}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9c3ba825-9890-578d-9db6-d3a5c2724c7e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9c3ba825-9890-578d-9db6-d3a5c2724c7e" :answer-field/id #uuid "9c3ba825-9890-578d-9db6-d3a5c2724c7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-53886b5d-6f70-5e43-94e2-1e5c1874216b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f7a62d43-c981-536a-8470-beb381af763b" "answer-09c558eb-fd0d-5210-be98-00e719c69f54" "answer-d2fd44e1-bc2d-5988-aa65-9c881a641c2d" "answer-5ac81716-290f-5f5e-ba9c-4a78de5c2654" "answer-53886b5d-6f70-5e43-94e2-1e5c1874216b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7a62d43-c981-536a-8470-beb381af763b" :answer/id #uuid "f7a62d43-c981-536a-8470-beb381af763b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{20}}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09c558eb-fd0d-5210-be98-00e719c69f54" :answer/id #uuid "09c558eb-fd0d-5210-be98-00e719c69f54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2fd44e1-bc2d-5988-aa65-9c881a641c2d" :answer/id #uuid "d2fd44e1-bc2d-5988-aa65-9c881a641c2d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{e}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ac81716-290f-5f5e-ba9c-4a78de5c2654" :answer/id #uuid "5ac81716-290f-5f5e-ba9c-4a78de5c2654" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{e}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-53886b5d-6f70-5e43-94e2-1e5c1874216b" :answer/id #uuid "53886b5d-6f70-5e43-94e2-1e5c1874216b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-062c010f-c635-5129-83e7-3b14e937de12" :question/id #uuid "062c010f-c635-5129-83e7-3b14e937de12" :question/math-academy-id "q-79392" :question/problem "Find the exact solution to $3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-4)-1=8.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-4)-1 & =8 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-4) & =9 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-4) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-4) & =3 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-4)} & ={e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-4)}=2x-4.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4 & ={e}^{3} \\\\ 2x & ={e}^{3}+4 \\\\ x & =\\frac{{e}^{3}+4}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3106eed6-8bd8-551a-ae54-4cf6a337b987"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3106eed6-8bd8-551a-ae54-4cf6a337b987" :answer-field/id #uuid "3106eed6-8bd8-551a-ae54-4cf6a337b987" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-46b90bb8-0fc7-5c5c-bad2-2fb0c93cdf89" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d7e54fd4-d7c6-5ca2-b825-fe7894787dd6" "answer-423c0019-e126-5d75-a32d-16e3969d0e8b" "answer-03cb6034-c7f0-53cf-a5c7-f386c1a926a6" "answer-46b90bb8-0fc7-5c5c-bad2-2fb0c93cdf89" "answer-a4210421-e445-52ec-9f6c-78182e46d202"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7e54fd4-d7c6-5ca2-b825-fe7894787dd6" :answer/id #uuid "d7e54fd4-d7c6-5ca2-b825-fe7894787dd6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2-{e}^{3}}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-423c0019-e126-5d75-a32d-16e3969d0e8b" :answer/id #uuid "423c0019-e126-5d75-a32d-16e3969d0e8b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4-{e}^{3}}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03cb6034-c7f0-53cf-a5c7-f386c1a926a6" :answer/id #uuid "03cb6034-c7f0-53cf-a5c7-f386c1a926a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}+1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46b90bb8-0fc7-5c5c-bad2-2fb0c93cdf89" :answer/id #uuid "46b90bb8-0fc7-5c5c-bad2-2fb0c93cdf89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}+4}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a4210421-e445-52ec-9f6c-78182e46d202" :answer/id #uuid "a4210421-e445-52ec-9f6c-78182e46d202" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}-2}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b08ed48d-cd63-5608-bd95-a1073f0e3d2b" :question/id #uuid "b08ed48d-cd63-5608-bd95-a1073f0e3d2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-100751" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=9-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$, then what is the value of  $x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify our equation by collecting the like logarithmic terms, and isolating the logarithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =9-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =9 \\\\ (1+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =9 \\\\ 6\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =9 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} & ={e}^{3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=x.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{3/2} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{{e}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9602e175-11e5-59b1-b720-16f8e03a65be"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9602e175-11e5-59b1-b720-16f8e03a65be" :answer-field/id #uuid "9602e175-11e5-59b1-b720-16f8e03a65be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5147f178-8ec1-5bea-a9a5-601c6745cf81" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1f7862de-d6b2-526e-b206-39720ffd01fb" "answer-ad0979f9-9199-570a-87cc-396dfa37d480" "answer-5147f178-8ec1-5bea-a9a5-601c6745cf81" "answer-c8a05064-e15b-5c40-9806-46b42919a4eb" "answer-236406ba-3828-526b-b8fd-cb0f5cdb7e72"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f7862de-d6b2-526e-b206-39720ffd01fb" :answer/id #uuid "1f7862de-d6b2-526e-b206-39720ffd01fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{{e}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad0979f9-9199-570a-87cc-396dfa37d480" :answer/id #uuid "ad0979f9-9199-570a-87cc-396dfa37d480" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{{e}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5147f178-8ec1-5bea-a9a5-601c6745cf81" :answer/id #uuid "5147f178-8ec1-5bea-a9a5-601c6745cf81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{{e}^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c8a05064-e15b-5c40-9806-46b42919a4eb" :answer/id #uuid "c8a05064-e15b-5c40-9806-46b42919a4eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-236406ba-3828-526b-b8fd-cb0f5cdb7e72" :answer/id #uuid "236406ba-3828-526b-b8fd-cb0f5cdb7e72" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e999e0ab-2ba5-58ed-8052-a9586c979824" :question/id #uuid "e999e0ab-2ba5-58ed-8052-a9586c979824" :question/math-academy-id "q-110775" :question/problem "If $3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+3=3,$ what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+3 & =3 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} & ={e}^{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=x.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nx={e}^{0}=1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-665803db-21ed-53a8-9497-9d47247879f3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-665803db-21ed-53a8-9497-9d47247879f3" :answer-field/id #uuid "665803db-21ed-53a8-9497-9d47247879f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e0c503b8-3b2b-5c25-9b53-fa466c49ee04" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e0c503b8-3b2b-5c25-9b53-fa466c49ee04" "answer-39668388-df55-5c1b-9911-4aa95c56cdd9" "answer-a316d92a-a701-5e92-bd9c-d1bddecf5a6c" "answer-146cac5a-4870-5181-a369-791a7c7c4c11" "answer-ec9c5137-c2c7-55cc-b9a1-73b832be0821"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0c503b8-3b2b-5c25-9b53-fa466c49ee04" :answer/id #uuid "e0c503b8-3b2b-5c25-9b53-fa466c49ee04" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-39668388-df55-5c1b-9911-4aa95c56cdd9" :answer/id #uuid "39668388-df55-5c1b-9911-4aa95c56cdd9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a316d92a-a701-5e92-bd9c-d1bddecf5a6c" :answer/id #uuid "a316d92a-a701-5e92-bd9c-d1bddecf5a6c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-146cac5a-4870-5181-a369-791a7c7c4c11" :answer/id #uuid "146cac5a-4870-5181-a369-791a7c7c4c11" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec9c5137-c2c7-55cc-b9a1-73b832be0821" :answer/id #uuid "ec9c5137-c2c7-55cc-b9a1-73b832be0821" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-41c20f80-b3b2-5088-bf8c-59a72906730d" :activity/id #uuid "41c20f80-b3b2-5088-bf8c-59a72906730d" :activity/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing the Natural Logarithm" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048741 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-3142a03a-104a-5f55-930a-5477fde300ba" "step-e9fb026a-3bec-5018-a15c-ff7fb93b10fd" "step-013f2eef-7094-5b40-95fe-1315ad5661d1" "step-00083c00-24dc-506e-a324-73b019d6c818" "step-17f79400-9491-54ee-b554-54208170f405"] :activity/first-step "step-3142a03a-104a-5f55-930a-5477fde300ba" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-3142a03a-104a-5f55-930a-5477fde300ba" :step/id #uuid "3142a03a-104a-5f55-930a-5477fde300ba" :step/content "question-c0394c3f-264a-5afa-ba91-982051eae5c0" :step/next "step-e9fb026a-3bec-5018-a15c-ff7fb93b10fd" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e9fb026a-3bec-5018-a15c-ff7fb93b10fd" :step/id #uuid "e9fb026a-3bec-5018-a15c-ff7fb93b10fd" :step/content "question-9bdc2bf5-cb58-518d-9b62-85ffcb2b1edc" :step/next "step-013f2eef-7094-5b40-95fe-1315ad5661d1" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-013f2eef-7094-5b40-95fe-1315ad5661d1" :step/id #uuid "013f2eef-7094-5b40-95fe-1315ad5661d1" :step/content "question-062c010f-c635-5129-83e7-3b14e937de12" :step/next "step-00083c00-24dc-506e-a324-73b019d6c818" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-00083c00-24dc-506e-a324-73b019d6c818" :step/id #uuid "00083c00-24dc-506e-a324-73b019d6c818" :step/content "question-b08ed48d-cd63-5608-bd95-a1073f0e3d2b" :step/next "step-17f79400-9491-54ee-b554-54208170f405" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-17f79400-9491-54ee-b554-54208170f405" :step/id #uuid "17f79400-9491-54ee-b554-54208170f405" :step/content "question-e999e0ab-2ba5-58ed-8052-a9586c979824" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064220 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-affed96b-3c3d-54a9-a279-f8432cb1a012"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-affed96b-3c3d-54a9-a279-f8432cb1a012" :question/id #uuid "affed96b-3c3d-54a9-a279-f8432cb1a012" :question/math-academy-id "q-114073" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{2}{5+20{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can write this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{2}{5+20{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{2}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $4{x}^{2}=(2x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{2}{5+20{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{1+(2x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5596b996-55c7-584d-a670-904b75102451"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5596b996-55c7-584d-a670-904b75102451" :answer-field/id #uuid "5596b996-55c7-584d-a670-904b75102451" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0d190ad0-5d86-58ee-99e2-6b1dcbd8e20d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b06e5be7-dbe2-5334-99a2-daeab5470c11" "answer-0d190ad0-5d86-58ee-99e2-6b1dcbd8e20d" "answer-c764df4f-a1d7-5e04-a973-c2800addafd3" "answer-03b1168a-ec72-53fd-aa75-2d246631fb21" "answer-7f9a665e-71e9-5ec8-bf10-4366e938cdb7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b06e5be7-dbe2-5334-99a2-daeab5470c11" :answer/id #uuid "b06e5be7-dbe2-5334-99a2-daeab5470c11" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d190ad0-5d86-58ee-99e2-6b1dcbd8e20d" :answer/id #uuid "0d190ad0-5d86-58ee-99e2-6b1dcbd8e20d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{20}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c764df4f-a1d7-5e04-a973-c2800addafd3" :answer/id #uuid "c764df4f-a1d7-5e04-a973-c2800addafd3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03b1168a-ec72-53fd-aa75-2d246631fb21" :answer/id #uuid "03b1168a-ec72-53fd-aa75-2d246631fb21" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f9a665e-71e9-5ec8-bf10-4366e938cdb7" :answer/id #uuid "7f9a665e-71e9-5ec8-bf10-4366e938cdb7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186320599 :answer-field/correct "answer-1222c3a4-de2b-5ccf-879e-6d55aee4c8b9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1222c3a4-de2b-5ccf-879e-6d55aee4c8b9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1222c3a4-de2b-5ccf-879e-6d55aee4c8b9" :answer/id #uuid "1222c3a4-de2b-5ccf-879e-6d55aee4c8b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi\\sqrt{3}}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-39c86382-08ce-590d-8116-eedcd38c705c" :activity/id #uuid "39c86382-08ce-590d-8116-eedcd38c705c" :activity/title "Integration by Substitution With Inverse Trigonometric Functions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048189 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-567dadd3-ca44-5bf9-8b98-d51883d57900" "step-e5a7421e-17cd-51fe-9d7d-db4bb3d13560" "step-3a8560ad-d3a4-5e23-a9c1-ad08b689d611" "step-9e5e11b8-5899-58e1-8dff-5ad152048b10" "step-edde14b3-9900-568d-8442-2bcf6680ae81"] :activity/first-step "step-567dadd3-ca44-5bf9-8b98-d51883d57900" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-567dadd3-ca44-5bf9-8b98-d51883d57900" :step/id #uuid "567dadd3-ca44-5bf9-8b98-d51883d57900" :step/content 17592186335977 :step/next "step-e5a7421e-17cd-51fe-9d7d-db4bb3d13560" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e5a7421e-17cd-51fe-9d7d-db4bb3d13560" :step/id #uuid "e5a7421e-17cd-51fe-9d7d-db4bb3d13560" :step/content 17592186064952 :step/next "step-3a8560ad-d3a4-5e23-a9c1-ad08b689d611" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3a8560ad-d3a4-5e23-a9c1-ad08b689d611" :step/id #uuid "3a8560ad-d3a4-5e23-a9c1-ad08b689d611" :step/content 17592186250172 :step/next "step-9e5e11b8-5899-58e1-8dff-5ad152048b10" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-9e5e11b8-5899-58e1-8dff-5ad152048b10" :step/id #uuid "9e5e11b8-5899-58e1-8dff-5ad152048b10" :step/content "question-affed96b-3c3d-54a9-a279-f8432cb1a012" :step/next "step-edde14b3-9900-568d-8442-2bcf6680ae81" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-edde14b3-9900-568d-8442-2bcf6680ae81" :step/id #uuid "edde14b3-9900-568d-8442-2bcf6680ae81" :step/content 17592186320917 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157437 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-543d7533-dccc-5fe7-9798-fd9513ac4953"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-543d7533-dccc-5fe7-9798-fd9513ac4953" :question/id #uuid "543d7533-dccc-5fe7-9798-fd9513ac4953" :question/math-academy-id "q-88456" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x+2}-32\\cdot {5}^{x+2}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x+2}-32\\cdot {5}^{x+2} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}-32)\\cdot {5}^{x+2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-32=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{x+2}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}-32=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-16) & =0 \\\\ 2(x+4)(x-4) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-4,4.$\n\n- The equation $2=0$ is not a possible solution because it's a false statement.\n\n- The equation ${5}^{x+2}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{x+2}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-4,4,$ and their product is $(-4)\\cdot 4=-16.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-000395f2-2229-5afc-a0c3-6b50cd88d3b5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-000395f2-2229-5afc-a0c3-6b50cd88d3b5" :answer-field/id #uuid "000395f2-2229-5afc-a0c3-6b50cd88d3b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-13c010e0-d904-51ec-b491-72fa13a1c722" :answer-field/choices ["answer-13c010e0-d904-51ec-b491-72fa13a1c722" "answer-7e6209ff-c890-5b77-aea9-3b062a5bffb3" "answer-1ab45d96-d124-5fb4-b8cc-95b11dfddc6c" "answer-b0fe6e5e-9156-5eb2-886b-f6d9bd66017f" "answer-c2efe1d1-723f-56a8-b37f-a809b02fb084"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13c010e0-d904-51ec-b491-72fa13a1c722" :answer/id #uuid "13c010e0-d904-51ec-b491-72fa13a1c722" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e6209ff-c890-5b77-aea9-3b062a5bffb3" :answer/id #uuid "7e6209ff-c890-5b77-aea9-3b062a5bffb3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ab45d96-d124-5fb4-b8cc-95b11dfddc6c" :answer/id #uuid "1ab45d96-d124-5fb4-b8cc-95b11dfddc6c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0fe6e5e-9156-5eb2-886b-f6d9bd66017f" :answer/id #uuid "b0fe6e5e-9156-5eb2-886b-f6d9bd66017f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2efe1d1-723f-56a8-b37f-a809b02fb084" :answer/id #uuid "c2efe1d1-723f-56a8-b37f-a809b02fb084" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157438 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-bb3bd545-e795-5869-ab36-2a8ac7b0ac72"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-bb3bd545-e795-5869-ab36-2a8ac7b0ac72" :question/id #uuid "bb3bd545-e795-5869-ab36-2a8ac7b0ac72" :question/math-academy-id "q-88461" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $4\\cdot {5}^{x-2}={x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x-2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot {5}^{x-2} & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x-2} \\\\ 4\\cdot {5}^{x-2}-{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x-2} & =0 \\\\ (4-{x}^{2}){5}^{x-2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n4-{x}^{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{x-2}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $4-{x}^{2}=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(2+x)(2-x)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-2,2.$\n\n- The equation ${5}^{x-2}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{x-2}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-2,2,$ and their product is $(-2)\\cdot 2=-4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2e621ebc-06c8-5c50-ae08-59bcabc99cdf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2e621ebc-06c8-5c50-ae08-59bcabc99cdf" :answer-field/id #uuid "2e621ebc-06c8-5c50-ae08-59bcabc99cdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-df115754-c961-559d-9269-701572e5321c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-df115754-c961-559d-9269-701572e5321c" "answer-70cc8f24-5b90-57ac-b62e-1362fb9e8618" "answer-949f3a69-bede-5a74-a0b5-be25455f3451" "answer-513cdd03-c241-5b39-91b6-0279ee268055" "answer-76c93d58-5244-5182-bfad-36ccd0a40c29"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df115754-c961-559d-9269-701572e5321c" :answer/id #uuid "df115754-c961-559d-9269-701572e5321c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70cc8f24-5b90-57ac-b62e-1362fb9e8618" :answer/id #uuid "70cc8f24-5b90-57ac-b62e-1362fb9e8618" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-949f3a69-bede-5a74-a0b5-be25455f3451" :answer/id #uuid "949f3a69-bede-5a74-a0b5-be25455f3451" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-513cdd03-c241-5b39-91b6-0279ee268055" :answer/id #uuid "513cdd03-c241-5b39-91b6-0279ee268055" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76c93d58-5244-5182-bfad-36ccd0a40c29" :answer/id #uuid "76c93d58-5244-5182-bfad-36ccd0a40c29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-e3578813-12a2-53c7-b590-c0aff695b404" :activity/id #uuid "e3578813-12a2-53c7-b590-c0aff695b404" :activity/title "Solving Exponential Equations Using the Zero-Product Property" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048423 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-1e124127-bb29-56d9-aea9-e096e5bceea6" "step-698684ba-c1ee-5c3c-b7f8-006e905d879a" "step-ab84e546-33f9-5443-89fa-b2f73b6f13b0"] :activity/first-step "step-1e124127-bb29-56d9-aea9-e096e5bceea6" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-1e124127-bb29-56d9-aea9-e096e5bceea6" :step/id #uuid "1e124127-bb29-56d9-aea9-e096e5bceea6" :step/content "question-543d7533-dccc-5fe7-9798-fd9513ac4953" :step/next "step-698684ba-c1ee-5c3c-b7f8-006e905d879a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-698684ba-c1ee-5c3c-b7f8-006e905d879a" :step/id #uuid "698684ba-c1ee-5c3c-b7f8-006e905d879a" :step/content 17592186354646 :step/next "step-ab84e546-33f9-5443-89fa-b2f73b6f13b0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ab84e546-33f9-5443-89fa-b2f73b6f13b0" :step/id #uuid "ab84e546-33f9-5443-89fa-b2f73b6f13b0" :step/content "question-bb3bd545-e795-5869-ab36-2a8ac7b0ac72" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186174124 :answer-field/choices 17592186174128] [:db/retract 17592186174124 :answer-field/choices 17592186174129] [:db/retract 17592186174124 :answer-field/choices 17592186174125] [:db/retract 17592186174124 :answer-field/choices 17592186174126] [:db/retract 17592186174124 :answer-field/choices 17592186174127] {:db/id 17592186176024 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-af0d631e-6f1f-57fd-bd84-ccd6cde384ee" "question-37e413b5-1ec6-5915-aa78-dfcf790b6b22"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-af0d631e-6f1f-57fd-bd84-ccd6cde384ee" :question/id #uuid "af0d631e-6f1f-57fd-bd84-ccd6cde384ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-126546" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $F(x,y)=⟨y+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},\\,x{y}^{2}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(y+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})\\,dx+x{y}^{2}\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/0c/0cb340ef33d4cc38081e3796b4c8e076f1499760c0a3ffeecb8123a41fefeec8.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ and $Q(x,y)=x{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(y+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})\\,dx+x{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-1)\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cf819579-d6fd-53c1-af84-bcfb66bf0607"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cf819579-d6fd-53c1-af84-bcfb66bf0607" :answer-field/id #uuid "cf819579-d6fd-53c1-af84-bcfb66bf0607" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1e31ec7e-c4a0-5af1-a007-a5f374623cde" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4697b16a-b954-5ce9-9fdc-2eb3d67c270a" "answer-4a79f07c-c8a1-549a-81cf-2a1164ba3d60" "answer-307eac12-8ffa-5b27-b7eb-9fa2f8ffbd12" "answer-656fd776-3d31-5e32-af9a-81ea6f3a0abf" "answer-1e31ec7e-c4a0-5af1-a007-a5f374623cde"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4697b16a-b954-5ce9-9fdc-2eb3d67c270a" :answer/id #uuid "4697b16a-b954-5ce9-9fdc-2eb3d67c270a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a79f07c-c8a1-549a-81cf-2a1164ba3d60" :answer/id #uuid "4a79f07c-c8a1-549a-81cf-2a1164ba3d60" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-307eac12-8ffa-5b27-b7eb-9fa2f8ffbd12" :answer/id #uuid "307eac12-8ffa-5b27-b7eb-9fa2f8ffbd12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta -r" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-656fd776-3d31-5e32-af9a-81ea6f3a0abf" :answer/id #uuid "656fd776-3d31-5e32-af9a-81ea6f3a0abf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -r" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e31ec7e-c4a0-5af1-a007-a5f374623cde" :answer/id #uuid "1e31ec7e-c4a0-5af1-a007-a5f374623cde" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -r" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176311 :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $F(x,y)=⟨x{y}^{2},\\,x+{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+(x+{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/d8/d831c7fb805d16ec7a2dc2462813ea79b78d888ed2121330ed5d823b590d3c4f.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=x+{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+(x+{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-2\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}r-{r}^{3}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186174124 :answer-field/correct "answer-da61f6d3-ed49-54d2-b863-5a31a5f2ff0f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e9bae659-b917-5899-868d-3dd4b4436e04" "answer-dce15c84-2fee-5b91-8d2e-70aabd4925cc" "answer-da61f6d3-ed49-54d2-b863-5a31a5f2ff0f" "answer-6763f71a-2f1e-5e1a-b5b0-80171e8077f0" "answer-eb7dfd07-1378-5021-b284-61af5aa9f83b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9bae659-b917-5899-868d-3dd4b4436e04" :answer/id #uuid "e9bae659-b917-5899-868d-3dd4b4436e04" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dce15c84-2fee-5b91-8d2e-70aabd4925cc" :answer/id #uuid "dce15c84-2fee-5b91-8d2e-70aabd4925cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da61f6d3-ed49-54d2-b863-5a31a5f2ff0f" :answer/id #uuid "da61f6d3-ed49-54d2-b863-5a31a5f2ff0f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6763f71a-2f1e-5e1a-b5b0-80171e8077f0" :answer/id #uuid "6763f71a-2f1e-5e1a-b5b0-80171e8077f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }-r{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb7dfd07-1378-5021-b284-61af5aa9f83b" :answer/id #uuid "eb7dfd07-1378-5021-b284-61af5aa9f83b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-37e413b5-1ec6-5915-aa78-dfcf790b6b22" :question/id #uuid "37e413b5-1ec6-5915-aa78-dfcf790b6b22" :question/math-academy-id "q-126519" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{y}^{3}\\,dx-{x}^{3}\\,dy={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/79/798665574b8f0e11d991bb0d934efd01bdeb65840c2e86498be4edbc59578439.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=-{x}^{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{3}\\,dx-{x}^{3}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-3\\cdot (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-3\\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}(-3{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}-3{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}-3{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-600ccac0-481d-5135-af9b-c1f28719ac01"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-600ccac0-481d-5135-af9b-c1f28719ac01" :answer-field/id #uuid "600ccac0-481d-5135-af9b-c1f28719ac01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9e873ca7-af38-5f44-9341-1fb3e1ea8712" :answer-field/choices ["answer-eb337268-12ae-51d7-ad27-55abc1397eb5" "answer-1f8a5c91-67b1-5ae9-83b6-a14d25c55b45" "answer-9e873ca7-af38-5f44-9341-1fb3e1ea8712" "answer-0c5d1032-d8df-5131-9315-d27ce3d12daa" "answer-46824a5c-5d89-5cc0-92ba-1b68ec6d9595"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb337268-12ae-51d7-ad27-55abc1397eb5" :answer/id #uuid "eb337268-12ae-51d7-ad27-55abc1397eb5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3{r}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f8a5c91-67b1-5ae9-83b6-a14d25c55b45" :answer/id #uuid "1f8a5c91-67b1-5ae9-83b6-a14d25c55b45" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e873ca7-af38-5f44-9341-1fb3e1ea8712" :answer/id #uuid "9e873ca7-af38-5f44-9341-1fb3e1ea8712" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3{r}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c5d1032-d8df-5131-9315-d27ce3d12daa" :answer/id #uuid "0c5d1032-d8df-5131-9315-d27ce3d12daa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3{r}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46824a5c-5d89-5cc0-92ba-1b68ec6d9595" :answer/id #uuid "46824a5c-5d89-5cc0-92ba-1b68ec6d9595" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176223 :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/de/deeb5648e328ecc93cf6c1de620e103da0f7677705d1e89e4654deff8d411f66.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=3y-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ and $Q(x,y)=7x+\\sqrt{y+5},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(3y-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,dx+(7x+\\sqrt{y+5})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7-3)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}4\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}4r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186416109 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+(-xy)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/e1/e1c053e1cc4b4304c1302da2c57278200931b84e4ba687269d0ea89a99b523b8.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x+y$ and $Q(x,y)=-xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+(-xy)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y-1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y-1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 6,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-y-1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}(-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r)\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186416112 :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/77/7724df0035511419dcb5117ba8aa337380325af22ee235d206a954aa12c62fe9.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={y}^{3}-x$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{3}+{e}^{1/y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{3}-x)\\,dx+({x}^{3}+{e}^{1/y})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}3({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186262637 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({y}^{2}+{x}^{2})\\,dx+(xy+y{e}^{y})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/23/232ced663dcac27ce2775af66dd2ef215ed937e0f4eb5e3e6fb008a3d82dc069.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={y}^{2}+{x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=xy+y{e}^{y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{2}+{x}^{2})\\,dx+(xy+y{e}^{y})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,-\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054628 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ae7dac11-f018-50fc-a556-53f9ec9e3fc0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ae7dac11-f018-50fc-a556-53f9ec9e3fc0" :question/id #uuid "ae7dac11-f018-50fc-a556-53f9ec9e3fc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-13587" :question/problem "If $f(x)=-3x-5$ and $g(x)=-5x+12,$ then $(f-g)(x)=$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(f-g)(x)$ means the difference of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\nTo find the difference of the two functions, we subtract $g(x)$ from $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f-g)(x) & =f(x)-g(x) \\\\  & =(-3x-5)-(-5x+12) \\\\  & =(-3x-(-5x))-(5+12) \\\\  & =2x-17\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-089d659a-8eb7-5bd6-b111-7794095c5418"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-089d659a-8eb7-5bd6-b111-7794095c5418" :answer-field/id #uuid "089d659a-8eb7-5bd6-b111-7794095c5418" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-21d18250-9a08-5c6e-8e4d-92e1fb245b3b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-91192fb3-a90d-56bb-9ce3-326544036112" "answer-48e6d633-a71c-59fd-93b7-8fd33e0ccda3" "answer-21d18250-9a08-5c6e-8e4d-92e1fb245b3b" "answer-f7bc7f4b-7fe5-5aae-93df-2598fb61aa24" "answer-84226c33-baa7-5e2e-8a05-7f74aaad1f98"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91192fb3-a90d-56bb-9ce3-326544036112" :answer/id #